{"job":{"id":20055418,"platform":"FieldNation","company":"Netcom Business IT Solutions","title":"NC18 - Site Survey & Installation Quote — Multi-Site Verkada Camera & PTP Wireless Deployment","description":{"html":"<p>We are deploying Verkada camera systems and point-to-point (PTP) wireless bridges in <strong>this commercial site</strong>.</p>\n<p>The attached layout/floor plan document shows the proposed camera positions (FCAM/MCAM), and general site footprint for the location, based on a preliminary remote design. This is a<strong>planning reference only</strong> — the technician must physically verify every position on-site, since actual mounting surfaces, obstructions, cable paths, and line-of-sight may differ from the layout.</p>\n<p>The technician performing this survey <strong>must also be the one performing the eventual installation</strong>, and must submit a firm installation quote based on their own findings — not based on the layout alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Scope of Work</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>1. IDF / Network Closet Identification</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Locate and confirm the IDF or network closet location at each site.</li>\n<li>Note distance from the IDF to each proposed camera location.</li>\n<li>Confirm available switch port capacity, rack space, and power (PoE availability) in the IDF.</li>\n<li>Photograph the IDF, existing rack, and patch panel/switch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. PTP Antenna Mounting &amp; Placement</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The PTP antennas are intended to establish wireless communication <strong>between two buildings/sites</strong>(building-to-building bridge) — confirm clear line-of-sight exists between the proposed mounting points.</li>\n<li>Identify and recommend the best mounting location for each PTP antenna (roofline, wall mount, mast/pole, parapet, etc.), factoring in line-of-sight, height, obstructions (trees, other structures), and structural feasibility of the mount.</li>\n<li>Note the exact height and orientation needed to achieve line-of-sight between paired buildings.</li>\n<li>Take photos from the proposed antenna mount location showing the line-of-sight toward the paired building/antenna.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Power for PTP Antennas</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Locate the nearest available power source for each antenna (or confirm if PoE injector/PoE run from IDF is feasible instead).</li>\n<li>Note distance from the antenna mount point to the power source and whether a new circuit, extension, or PoE run would be required.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4. Camera Wiring Path Assessment (per camera location)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>For every camera position on the layout, determine whether a clear wiring path already exists (e.g., accessible ceiling, existing conduit, cable tray).</li>\n<li>If no clear path exists, determine:</li>\n<li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Whether conduit installation is required</li>\n<li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Whether the run can be done <strong>without conduit</strong> (e.g., protected interior spaces, existing raceway) or if conduit is mandatory per the run conditions</li>\n<li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Whether the run should go <strong>inside the building</strong> (interior ceiling/wall path) or will require an <strong>exterior/outdoor run</strong> (surface-mounted conduit on exterior wall, roof penetration, etc.)</li>\n<li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The recommended routing/method for each run (attic space, drop ceiling, surface mount, core drill, roof penetration, etc.)</li>\n<li>Measure and record the <strong>estimated linear footage of conduit</strong> needed per camera run, broken out by interior vs. exterior conduit if both are needed.</li>\n<li>Note any wall/roof penetrations required and their locations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>5. Documentation Requirements (mandatory for every site)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Detailed <strong>photos</strong> of: each camera mounting location, each proposed antenna mount location, the IDF, all proposed conduit paths, any obstructions or problem areas, and the exterior building line-of-sight between paired sites.</li>\n<li><strong>Video walkthrough</strong> of each site is preferred in addition to photos, especially for wiring paths.</li>\n<li><strong>Written measurements</strong>: cable run lengths (estimated), conduit lengths, mounting heights, distances from IDF to cameras/antennas.</li>\n<li>Notes on <strong>mounting surface type</strong> for every camera and antenna (drywall, brick, metal siding, wood fascia, EIFS, etc.) since this affects hardware and labor.</li>\n<li>Any site-specific access issues (locked areas, tenant coordination needed, ladder/lift requirements, roof access restrictions, etc.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>Quote Requirements</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>The technician's quote (submitted after the survey) must include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Labor cost</strong> (broken out per site if possible)</li>\n<li><strong>Material cost</strong> (cameras/mounts already known — focus on conduit, connectors, mounting hardware, antenna brackets, PoE injectors, cable, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>Estimated number of technicians required</strong> to complete the full install</li>\n<li><strong>Estimated total labor hours</strong> for the entire project</li>\n<li><strong>Estimated project duration</strong> (number of days/visits to complete installation, not just the survey)</li>\n<li><strong>Any anticipated additional equipment or materials</strong> not already covered in the base camera package — e.g., lifts/ladders for high mounts, roof penetration kits, weatherproof enclosures, additional PoE switches, exterior-rated conduit, antenna masts, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Additional Technician Requirements</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Technician must be comfortable working at height (roofline/antenna mounting) and have access to appropriate fall protection/ladder equipment during the survey to physically inspect mount points — not just view from ground level.</li>\n<li>Technician should flag any site where line-of-sight for the PTP bridge appears obstructed or questionable, since this may require a different antenna location or an additional relay point.</li>\n<li><strong>Incomplete submissions will be returned.</strong>Notes, photos, and measurements must be thorough enough that our team can validate the quote without needing a second site visit. Vague notes (e.g., \"looks fine,\" \"needs conduit\") without measurements, photos, or specifics on interior/exterior routing will not be accepted.</li>\n</ul>","markdown":"We are deploying Verkada camera systems and point-to-point (PTP) wireless bridges in **this commercial site**.\n\nThe attached layout/floor plan document shows the proposed camera positions (FCAM/MCAM), and general site footprint for the location, based on a preliminary remote design. This is a**planning reference only** — the technician must physically verify every position on-site, since actual mounting surfaces, obstructions, cable paths, and line-of-sight may differ from the layout.\n\nThe technician performing this survey **must also be the one performing the eventual installation**, and must submit a firm installation quote based on their own findings — not based on the layout alone.\n\n**Scope of Work**\n\n**1. IDF / Network Closet Identification**\n\n- Locate and confirm the IDF or network closet location at each site.\n- Note distance from the IDF to each proposed camera location.\n- Confirm available switch port capacity, rack space, and power (PoE availability) in the IDF.\n- Photograph the IDF, existing rack, and patch panel/switch.\n\n**2. PTP Antenna Mounting & Placement**\n\n- The PTP antennas are intended to establish wireless communication **between two buildings/sites**(building-to-building bridge) — confirm clear line-of-sight exists between the proposed mounting points.\n- Identify and recommend the best mounting location for each PTP antenna (roofline, wall mount, mast/pole, parapet, etc.), factoring in line-of-sight, height, obstructions (trees, other structures), and structural feasibility of the mount.\n- Note the exact height and orientation needed to achieve line-of-sight between paired buildings.\n- Take photos from the proposed antenna mount location showing the line-of-sight toward the paired building/antenna.\n\n**3. Power for PTP Antennas**\n\n- Locate the nearest available power source for each antenna (or confirm if PoE injector/PoE run from IDF is feasible instead).\n- Note distance from the antenna mount point to the power source and whether a new circuit, extension, or PoE run would be required.\n\n**4. Camera Wiring Path Assessment (per camera location)**\n\n- For every camera position on the layout, determine whether a clear wiring path already exists (e.g., accessible ceiling, existing conduit, cable tray).\n- If no clear path exists, determine:\n- Whether conduit installation is required\n- Whether the run can be done **without conduit** (e.g., protected interior spaces, existing raceway) or if conduit is mandatory per the run conditions\n- Whether the run should go **inside the building** (interior ceiling/wall path) or will require an **exterior/outdoor run** (surface-mounted conduit on exterior wall, roof penetration, etc.)\n- The recommended routing/method for each run (attic space, drop ceiling, surface mount, core drill, roof penetration, etc.)\n- Measure and record the **estimated linear footage of conduit** needed per camera run, broken out by interior vs. exterior conduit if both are needed.\n- Note any wall/roof penetrations required and their locations.\n\n**5. Documentation Requirements (mandatory for every site)**\n\n- Detailed **photos** of: each camera mounting location, each proposed antenna mount location, the IDF, all proposed conduit paths, any obstructions or problem areas, and the exterior building line-of-sight between paired sites.\n- **Video walkthrough** of each site is preferred in addition to photos, especially for wiring paths.\n- **Written measurements**: cable run lengths (estimated), conduit lengths, mounting heights, distances from IDF to cameras/antennas.\n- Notes on **mounting surface type** for every camera and antenna (drywall, brick, metal siding, wood fascia, EIFS, etc.) since this affects hardware and labor.\n- Any site-specific access issues (locked areas, tenant coordination needed, ladder/lift requirements, roof access restrictions, etc.)\n\n**Quote Requirements**\n\nThe technician's quote (submitted after the survey) must include:\n\n- **Labor cost** (broken out per site if possible)\n- **Material cost** (cameras/mounts already known — focus on conduit, connectors, mounting hardware, antenna brackets, PoE injectors, cable, etc.)\n- **Estimated number of technicians required** to complete the full install\n- **Estimated total labor hours** for the entire project\n- **Estimated project duration** (number of days/visits to complete installation, not just the survey)\n- **Any anticipated additional equipment or materials** not already covered in the base camera package — e.g., lifts/ladders for high mounts, roof penetration kits, weatherproof enclosures, additional PoE switches, exterior-rated conduit, antenna masts, etc.\n\nAdditional Technician Requirements\n\n- Technician must be comfortable working at height (roofline/antenna mounting) and have access to appropriate fall protection/ladder equipment during the survey to physically inspect mount points — not just view from ground level.\n- Technician should flag any site where line-of-sight for the PTP bridge appears obstructed or questionable, since this may require a different antenna location or an additional relay point.\n- **Incomplete submissions will be returned.**Notes, photos, and measurements must be thorough enough that our team can validate the quote without needing a second site visit. Vague notes (e.g., \"looks fine,\" \"needs conduit\") without measurements, photos, or specifics on interior/exterior routing will not be accepted.","actions":[]},"isRequestedWindow":false,"time":{"start":"2026-10-07T10:00","end":"2026-10-07T12:00"},"payRange":{"min":200,"max":200},"payType":"fixed","payStructure":null,"hourlyRate":0,"estLaborHours":2,"distance":40.64528042438343},"result":{"eligible":false,"counterOffer":null,"reason":"PAYMENT_BELOW_MINIMUM","rejectDetails":"📅 Requested schedule unavailable; a later date was considered.\nAlternative: Oct 9, 4:30 PM (Eastern)\n💸 Alternative-date minimum: $300 · listed maximum: $200\nRejected: pay does not meet the alternative-date minimum."},"availableBlocks":[{"start":"2026-10-07T20:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-08T14:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-10-08T22:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-09T00:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-10-09T13:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-09T14:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-10-09T20:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z"}],"busyBlocks":[{"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00.000Z","summary":"Vika Ortodont"},{"start":"2026-10-07T16:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-07T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"DMV"},{"start":"2026-10-09T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-09T17:00:00.000Z","summary":"Granite"},{"start":"2026-10-09T17:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-09T20:30:00.000Z","summary":"Vika USCIS biometry"},{"start":"2026-10-08T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-08T22:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 05809722 - VEG - Cary NC - Managed Starlink Survey (WM)"},{"start":"2026-10-15T18:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-15T21:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 05980771-The Edition on Oberlin-EPIK Survey (WM)"},{"start":"2026-10-07T13:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-07T17:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 Equipment Install | Priority 3 | 10/07/2026 | Hard Start (WM)"},{"start":"2026-10-12T13:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-12T16:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 05999131-Durham - Site #9927-EPIK Survey and Install (WM)"},{"start":"2026-10-09T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-09T17:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 04546900 Preiss Co View at Legacy Oaks Cutover/Test Install Wizard (WM)"},{"start":"2026-10-15T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-15T17:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 02207602 + Swarovksi - Swarovski #63 + epik cutover / revisit (WM)"},{"start":"2026-10-15T17:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-15T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 05974915-The Edition on Rosemary-EPIK Survey (WM)"},{"start":"2026-10-08T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-08T17:00:00.000Z","summary":"Weitz LVL12 - Sensor Maintenance & Gateway Installation"},{"start":"2026-10-05T16:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:30:00.000Z","summary":"Network Cable Technician – Low Voltage Tech 1 (AKIRA Crabtree Valley)"}],"action":"SKIP","ts":1791310994628}
{"job":{"id":20116731,"platform":"FieldNation","company":"ASR tech","title":"Perform Maintenance on an Automated Package Locker System (MUST HAVE ALL REQUIRED TOOLS)","description":{"html":"<p><strong><em>**PLEASE&nbsp;<u>READ</u>&nbsp;THE ENTIRE WORK ORDER BEFORE ACCEPTING*</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>*PLEASE&nbsp;<u>READ&nbsp;</u>PROBLEM DESCRIPTION/SCOPE OF WORK BEFORE ACCEPTING*</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>*PLEASE&nbsp;<u>READ&nbsp;</u>TOOL REQUIREMENT BEFORE ACCEPTING**</em></strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>**PROBLEM DESCRIPTION/SCOPE OF WORK**</u></strong></p><p><strong><em><u>&nbsp;The following has been reported:</u></em></strong></p><p><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Outdoor Zhilai - -</span></p><p><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">SYSTEM DOWN || Install Revisit - Once onsite, Text Scott at&nbsp;(949) 393-1241. If unavailable, call Field Service line&nbsp;949-317-4169</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">- No parts sent out</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">- Requesting tech for 10/7 9:00 a.m EST</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">- Need to: troubleshoot system offline after recent installation. Test network connection and verify networking cables are connected correctly in control tower </span></p><p><strong><em>Ensure the system is fully functional. General Linux, electrical, and mechanical knowledge is required, but technical support will guide you through most of the process. Professionalism and attention to detail are required for this client.&nbsp;&nbsp;Please be mindful of how you are representing ASR and yourself.&nbsp;&nbsp;</em></strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>**TOOL REQUIREMENT**ALL TOOLS LISTED ARE REQUIRED**</u></strong></p><p><strong>Government issued Identification</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard Technicians tool kit</strong></li><li><strong>Precision screwdriver set</strong></li><li><strong>Rubber Mallet</strong></li><li><strong>4' ladder</strong></li><li><strong>5\" round reusable hard surface sliders (or similar for floor protection)</strong></li><li><strong>Laptop w/ HyperTerminal or Putty, must be able to connect to CAT5 Cable and runs Windows XP or higher</strong></li><li><strong>Laptop must have Team Viewer</strong></li><li><strong>Drill/Driver</strong></li><li><strong>Laptop Charger</strong></li><li><strong>Wired USB Keyboard</strong></li><li><strong>6ft Crossover patch cable</strong></li><li><strong>6ft Standard patch cable</strong></li><li><strong>22-16 AWG&nbsp;wire splicing connectors</strong></li><li><strong>Silicone caulking (for end of self-tapping screws)</strong></li><li><strong>Crimper/Stripper tool</strong></li><li><strong>Punch Down tool (used to terminate keystone jacks)</strong></li><li><strong>Smart Phone with ability to access e-mail and open a mobile web page, Camera, Text</strong></li><li><strong>Smart Phone Charger</strong></li><li><strong>Digital Camera or the Smart Phone Camera (Must upload all photos of equipment worked on)</strong></li><li><strong>Dry Cloth/Towel and non-abrasive cleaner (wipe down lockers before leaving site)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Download, print, review, and bring the attached PDFs to site:&nbsp;</strong></p><ul><li>ServiceCallChecklist rev1_1.pdf</li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>SCOPE OF WORK MAY include open all box test, redelivering all packages, check for jammed/disabled doors, etc.</strong></p><p><strong>Some issues may take longer than expected.&nbsp;</strong></p><p>Potential issues can include any or all of the following:</p><ul><li>Jammed Door</li><li>Controller Unit Replacement</li><li>Sub Board Replacement</li><li>Solenoid Replacement</li><li>Clear Box/Redeliver Packages</li><li>If parts are to be returned, please use the prepaid return label provided</li><li>IMPORTANT:Please Take Photos of the equipment and parts before packing and returning</li></ul><p><strong><em><u>Professionalism, attention to detail, and customer care are required for this client, Customer Service skills are a must!&nbsp;Any discussion of prior work performed by anyone is not allowed. This may be reported back to Parcel and ASR. It may be interpreted as unprofessional behavior.</u></em></strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>ASR PROFESSIONALISM REQUIREMENTS</u></strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>Technician should NOT discuss any scheduling or payment issues with Parcel Pending support desk under any circumstances. Any scheduling or payment issues MUST be discussed with ASR.&nbsp;&nbsp;</u></strong></p><p><strong><u>If the customer reaches out directly to you, please refer them back to ASR.</u></strong></p><ul><li>Perform excellent customer service. Be friendly, communicate, and assure them they're in good hands.&nbsp;</li><li>Dress code is casual business attire with no logos.</li><li>Do not share any pricing information with client.&nbsp;</li><li>Do not leave personal business information or contact information with client.</li><li>Technician needs to ensure that each site is a DRUG-FREE WORKPLACE</li><li>The technician needs to ensure they do not smell or reek of odors or other substances.</li></ul><p><strong><u>ASR Services intends to help provide a safe and drug-free work environment for our clients.</u></strong></p><p><strong><u>The Company explicitly prohibits:</u></strong></p><p><strong><u>The use, possession, solicitation for, or sale of narcotics or other illegal drugs, alcohol, or prescription medication without a prescription on customer premises or while performing an assignment.</u></strong></p><p><strong><em><u>ASR Project Number: 887</u></em></strong></p><p><strong><u>STEP ONE:</u></strong></p><p><strong>CHECK-IN: With Parcel Pending, be mindful as you are in contact with the customer directly (your contact name and number is listed under the Problem Description)&nbsp;Ask if any Parts were shipped to site and who has the parts onsite. DO NOT PERFORM ANY WORK&nbsp;until you have contacted Parcel Pending.&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>Please contact our customer when you have arrived onsite.</strong></p><p><strong>***Always Leave a message if your contact does not answer and then call their Field Service line&nbsp;</strong><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><del>************</del></span>***</p><p><strong><u>STEP TWO:</u></strong></p><p><strong>CHECK-IN:On Field Nation - Please do not check in on&nbsp;Field Nation until you have checked in with Parcel Pending.&nbsp;​Your check-in/out times MUST match what Parcel Pending provides us.</strong></p><p><strong><u>STEP THREE:</u></strong></p><p><strong>LOCATE: Leasing/Rental Office, General Contractors Office, Concierge, Front Desk etc. Once in contact, ask for parts, if they were shipped.&nbsp;You would have this information from your contact in step one.</strong></p><p><strong><u>STEP FOUR:</u></strong></p><p><strong>FOLLOW: Direction from Parcel Pending.&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong><u>STEP FIVE:</u></strong></p><p><strong>REPORT: PROBLEMS/ISSUES&nbsp;BEFORE LEAVING SITE and&nbsp;<u>CHECK-OUT.</u>&nbsp;&nbsp;Please do NOT check out on Field Nation until you have checked out with Parcel Pending.&nbsp;​(Number and tech support name will be under the Problem Description</strong>)&nbsp;&nbsp;<strong>Your check-in/out times MUST match what our customer provides us.</strong></p><p><strong><u>STEP SIX:</u></strong></p><p><strong>SUBMIT</strong>: W<strong>ork order for payment within 24 hours of scheduled date.&nbsp;</strong>Please provide&nbsp;<strong>detailed closing notes</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>timeline&nbsp;</strong>when submitting this work order for payment.</p><p><strong><em>The name of the individual accepting this work must be the actual person who arrives on site and if we determine there was a substitution without prior notice, we will not pay for the work performed.&nbsp;&nbsp;</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>Be sure to notify ASR by phone and message in work order prior to tech arriving onsite.&nbsp;</em></strong></p><p><strong>ACCEPTING THIS WORK ORDER MEANS THAT YOU WILL HAVE ALL OF THE REQUIRED TOOLS.&nbsp;ALSO, HAVE READ AND UNDERSTAND THE SCOPE OF WORK.&nbsp;&nbsp;PLEASE UPDATE YOUR ETA AS SOON AS YOU ARE ASSIGNED. YOU WILL BE HELD RESPONSIBLE FOR ARRIVING ON THE SCHEDULED DATE AND TIME. ANY VIOLATIONS WILL BE ESCALATED TO FIELD NATION.&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong><em>Parcel Pending and Site may provide a report card based on professionalism and understanding of work being done.&nbsp;</em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Tech should NOT discuss payment or scheduling discrepancies with Parcel Pending, Site Contact or anyone but ASR (NO exceptions)</strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong>***Please CALL Rodrigo R. 949-556-3604</strong>&nbsp;for any general questions/information.***</p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>ONSITE DETAILS &amp; CONDUCT</u></strong></p><ul><li><strong>If you are unable to make it to site, you must call/text Rodrigo </strong>(Phone number in Confidential Information)</li><li><strong>Work Order is subject to Penalty Fee at Buyers Discretion.&nbsp;</strong></li><li><strong>You will be representing ASR Services when communicating with the end user or Parcel Pending support.</strong></li><li><strong>All conversation on site should be kept to business related items and not personal matters.&nbsp;</strong></li><li><strong>Resolve the issues using the documentation provided with support from Technical Support.</strong></li><li><strong>Do not access the \"System Maintenance Screen\" unless instructed by Technical Support.</strong></li><li><strong><u>Upon completion of service:&nbsp;KEY NEEDS TO BE IN SECURE LOCATION</u></strong></li><li><strong><u>a: please return keys to MOD onsite. Will need first and last name of keyholder (this info is required for release from site and Work Order closure).</u></strong></li><li><strong><u>b: If no onsite personnel are onsite/available, ask Parcel Pending Support/ASR Support Team member with whom you are working for direction on where keys should be left.&nbsp;</u></strong></li><li><strong>If you are requested to do anything out side of the scope of work, including returning to site, or dealing with a DOA, you must call/text Rodrigo.&nbsp;</strong>(Phone number in Confidential Information)</li><li><strong>Tech must provide photos of any equipment that is worked on.</strong></li><li><strong>Tech must fill out the ServiceCallChecklist rev1_1.pdf COMPLETELY and have it signed by the property manager</strong></li><li><strong>Tech must note arrival and departure times on sign off sheet, document any site delays and requests that are made out of the scope of work in the closing notes.</strong></li><li><strong>During completion, tech must provide any photos, closing notes, open issues, service call checklist, sign off sheet, and name of person that released tech from site.</strong></li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong><u>RETURNING EQUIPMENT</u></strong></p><p><strong>If any equipment needs to be returned to Parcel Pending after completion please take a photo of equipment or parts before leaving site (damaged or undamaged) to ensure responsibility.&nbsp;A return label will be in the shipping pouch behind the original shipping label.</strong></p><p><strong>If a return label was not provided, please contact Parcel Pending immediately to obtain BEFORE leaving site, they will email a return label to you.</strong></p><p><strong>***A release code will be provided to you once tracking shows that parts are on the way back to our customer***</strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>PAYMENT</u></strong></p><p>2 hours on site will be paid at a fixed rate of $85 with a potential of up to 2 additional hours paid in 15 min intervals @ $40/hr.</p><p><strong>Some issues may take long.&nbsp;Any time on site, after 2 hours, will need to be approved by Parcel Pending.</strong></p><p><strong>Your check in/out times on this work order must match what our customer provides.</strong></p><p>Site abort where technician does not go to site, but within 24 hrs $30.00</p><p>Site abort with technician onsite – determined by time spent at site.</p><p>Cancellation with more than 24 hrs notice will be paid $0.</p>","markdown":"***\\*\\*PLEASE READ THE ENTIRE WORK ORDER BEFORE ACCEPTING\\****\n\n***\\*PLEASE READ PROBLEM DESCRIPTION/SCOPE OF WORK BEFORE ACCEPTING\\****\n\n***\\*PLEASE READ TOOL REQUIREMENT BEFORE ACCEPTING\\*\\****\n\n**\\*\\*PROBLEM DESCRIPTION/SCOPE OF WORK\\*\\***\n\n ***The following has been reported:***\n\nOutdoor Zhilai - -\n\nSYSTEM DOWN || Install Revisit - Once onsite, Text Scott at (949) 393-1241. If unavailable, call Field Service line 949-317-4169\n\n  \n\\\n\n\\- No parts sent out\n\n  \n\\\n\n\\- Requesting tech for 10/7 9:00 a.m EST\n\n  \n\\\n\n\\- Need to: troubleshoot system offline after recent installation. Test network connection and verify networking cables are connected correctly in control tower\n\n***Ensure the system is fully functional. General Linux, electrical, and mechanical knowledge is required, but technical support will guide you through most of the process. Professionalism and attention to detail are required for this client. Please be mindful of how you are representing ASR and yourself.***\n\n**\\*\\*TOOL REQUIREMENT\\*\\*ALL TOOLS LISTED ARE REQUIRED\\*\\***\n\n**Government issued Identification**\n\n- **Standard Technicians tool kit**\n- **Precision screwdriver set**\n- **Rubber Mallet**\n- **4' ladder**\n- **5\" round reusable hard surface sliders (or similar for floor protection)**\n- **Laptop w/ HyperTerminal or Putty, must be able to connect to CAT5 Cable and runs Windows XP or higher**\n- **Laptop must have Team Viewer**\n- **Drill/Driver**\n- **Laptop Charger**\n- **Wired USB Keyboard**\n- **6ft Crossover patch cable**\n- **6ft Standard patch cable**\n- **22-16 AWG wire splicing connectors**\n- **Silicone caulking (for end of self-tapping screws)**\n- **Crimper/Stripper tool**\n- **Punch Down tool (used to terminate keystone jacks)**\n- **Smart Phone with ability to access e-mail and open a mobile web page, Camera, Text**\n- **Smart Phone Charger**\n- **Digital Camera or the Smart Phone Camera (Must upload all photos of equipment worked on)**\n- **Dry Cloth/Towel and non-abrasive cleaner (wipe down lockers before leaving site)**\n\n**Download, print, review, and bring the attached PDFs to site:**\n\n- ServiceCallChecklist rev1\\_1.pdf\n\n**SCOPE OF WORK MAY include open all box test, redelivering all packages, check for jammed/disabled doors, etc.**\n\n**Some issues may take longer than expected.**\n\nPotential issues can include any or all of the following:\n\n- Jammed Door\n- Controller Unit Replacement\n- Sub Board Replacement\n- Solenoid Replacement\n- Clear Box/Redeliver Packages\n- If parts are to be returned, please use the prepaid return label provided\n- IMPORTANT:Please Take Photos of the equipment and parts before packing and returning\n\n***Professionalism, attention to detail, and customer care are required for this client, Customer Service skills are a must! Any discussion of prior work performed by anyone is not allowed. This may be reported back to Parcel and ASR. It may be interpreted as unprofessional behavior.***\n\n**ASR PROFESSIONALISM REQUIREMENTS**\n\n**Technician should NOT discuss any scheduling or payment issues with Parcel Pending support desk under any circumstances. Any scheduling or payment issues MUST be discussed with ASR.**\n\n**If the customer reaches out directly to you, please refer them back to ASR.**\n\n- Perform excellent customer service. Be friendly, communicate, and assure them they're in good hands.\n- Dress code is casual business attire with no logos.\n- Do not share any pricing information with client.\n- Do not leave personal business information or contact information with client.\n- Technician needs to ensure that each site is a DRUG-FREE WORKPLACE\n- The technician needs to ensure they do not smell or reek of odors or other substances.\n\n**ASR Services intends to help provide a safe and drug-free work environment for our clients.**\n\n**The Company explicitly prohibits:**\n\n**The use, possession, solicitation for, or sale of narcotics or other illegal drugs, alcohol, or prescription medication without a prescription on customer premises or while performing an assignment.**\n\n***ASR Project Number: 887***\n\n**STEP ONE:**\n\n**CHECK-IN: With Parcel Pending, be mindful as you are in contact with the customer directly (your contact name and number is listed under the Problem Description) Ask if any Parts were shipped to site and who has the parts onsite. DO NOT PERFORM ANY WORK until you have contacted Parcel Pending.**\n\n**Please contact our customer when you have arrived onsite.**\n\n**\\*\\*\\*Always Leave a message if your contact does not answer and then call their Field Service line** \\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\\*\n\n**STEP TWO:**\n\n**CHECK-IN:On Field Nation - Please do not check in on Field Nation until you have checked in with Parcel Pending. ​Your check-in/out times MUST match what Parcel Pending provides us.**\n\n**STEP THREE:**\n\n**LOCATE: Leasing/Rental Office, General Contractors Office, Concierge, Front Desk etc. Once in contact, ask for parts, if they were shipped. You would have this information from your contact in step one.**\n\n**STEP FOUR:**\n\n**FOLLOW: Direction from Parcel Pending.**\n\n**STEP FIVE:**\n\n**REPORT: PROBLEMS/ISSUES BEFORE LEAVING SITE and CHECK-OUT. Please do NOT check out on Field Nation until you have checked out with Parcel Pending. ​(Number and tech support name will be under the Problem Description**) **Your check-in/out times MUST match what our customer provides us.**\n\n**STEP SIX:**\n\n**SUBMIT**: W**ork order for payment within 24 hours of scheduled date.** Please provide **detailed closing notes** and **timeline** when submitting this work order for payment.\n\n***The name of the individual accepting this work must be the actual person who arrives on site and if we determine there was a substitution without prior notice, we will not pay for the work performed.***\n\n***Be sure to notify ASR by phone and message in work order prior to tech arriving onsite.***\n\n**ACCEPTING THIS WORK ORDER MEANS THAT YOU WILL HAVE ALL OF THE REQUIRED TOOLS. ALSO, HAVE READ AND UNDERSTAND THE SCOPE OF WORK. PLEASE UPDATE YOUR ETA AS SOON AS YOU ARE ASSIGNED. YOU WILL BE HELD RESPONSIBLE FOR ARRIVING ON THE SCHEDULED DATE AND TIME. ANY VIOLATIONS WILL BE ESCALATED TO FIELD NATION.**\n\n***Parcel Pending and Site may provide a report card based on professionalism and understanding of work being done.***\n\n**Tech should NOT discuss payment or scheduling discrepancies with Parcel Pending, Site Contact or anyone but ASR (NO exceptions)**\n\n**\\*\\*\\*Please CALL Rodrigo R. 949-556-3604** for any general questions/information.\\*\\*\\*\n\n**ONSITE DETAILS & CONDUCT**\n\n- **If you are unable to make it to site, you must call/text Rodrigo** (Phone number in Confidential Information)\n- **Work Order is subject to Penalty Fee at Buyers Discretion.**\n- **You will be representing ASR Services when communicating with the end user or Parcel Pending support.**\n- **All conversation on site should be kept to business related items and not personal matters.**\n- **Resolve the issues using the documentation provided with support from Technical Support.**\n- **Do not access the \"System Maintenance Screen\" unless instructed by Technical Support.**\n- **Upon completion of service: KEY NEEDS TO BE IN SECURE LOCATION**\n- **a: please return keys to MOD onsite. Will need first and last name of keyholder (this info is required for release from site and Work Order closure).**\n- **b: If no onsite personnel are onsite/available, ask Parcel Pending Support/ASR Support Team member with whom you are working for direction on where keys should be left.**\n- **If you are requested to do anything out side of the scope of work, including returning to site, or dealing with a DOA, you must call/text Rodrigo.** (Phone number in Confidential Information)\n- **Tech must provide photos of any equipment that is worked on.**\n- **Tech must fill out the ServiceCallChecklist rev1\\_1.pdf COMPLETELY and have it signed by the property manager**\n- **Tech must note arrival and departure times on sign off sheet, document any site delays and requests that are made out of the scope of work in the closing notes.**\n- **During completion, tech must provide any photos, closing notes, open issues, service call checklist, sign off sheet, and name of person that released tech from site.**\n\n**RETURNING EQUIPMENT**\n\n**If any equipment needs to be returned to Parcel Pending after completion please take a photo of equipment or parts before leaving site (damaged or undamaged) to ensure responsibility. A return label will be in the shipping pouch behind the original shipping label.**\n\n**If a return label was not provided, please contact Parcel Pending immediately to obtain BEFORE leaving site, they will email a return label to you.**\n\n**\\*\\*\\*A release code will be provided to you once tracking shows that parts are on the way back to our customer\\*\\*\\***\n\n**PAYMENT**\n\n2 hours on site will be paid at a fixed rate of $85 with a potential of up to 2 additional hours paid in 15 min intervals @ $40/hr.\n\n**Some issues may take long. Any time on site, after 2 hours, will need to be approved by Parcel Pending.**\n\n**Your check in/out times on this work order must match what our customer provides.**\n\nSite abort where technician does not go to site, but within 24 hrs $30.00\n\nSite abort with technician onsite – determined by time spent at site.\n\nCancellation with more than 24 hrs notice will be paid $0.","actions":[]},"isRequestedWindow":true,"time":{"start":"2026-10-07T09:00","end":"2026-10-07T12:00","latestStart":"2026-10-07T14:00"},"payRange":{"min":85,"max":125},"payType":"bl