{"job":{"id":20221011,"platform":"FieldNation","company":"Gigstreem","title":"Cortland Brier Creek - Redundant Circuit Patch-In, Turn-Up & Power Validation- #326718","description":{"html":"<p><strong>Gigstreem is a national Internet service provider delivering high-quality internet services to residential and commercial properties across the country.</strong></p>Work Order Details<p><strong>Property:</strong> <strong>Address:</strong> 7920 Oak Estate St, Raleigh, NC 27617</p><p><strong>Customer:</strong> Cortland</p><p><strong>Work Location:</strong> LCP/MDF – Main Network Equipment Room</p>Scope of Work (SOW)<p>Dispatch an experienced Field Nation network technician to <strong>Cortland Brier Creek</strong> to complete the redundant circuit patch-in and Turn-Up (TTU), working directly with an assigned Gigstreem Engineer remotely.</p><p>The technician must physically connect the redundant circuit to the designated Edge-Router port, assist Engineering with circuit activation and testing, and complete the required visual power provisioning inspections.</p><p><strong>IMPORTANT: The technician MUST arrive with a Windows laptop with Winbox and PuTTY installed, an RJ45 Ethernet cable, and an RJ45-to-USB console cable compatible with MikroTik equipment.</strong></p>1. Site Access &amp; Initial Inspection<ul><li>Contact the onsite property contact at least 30 minutes before arrival to provide an accurate ETA.</li><li>Check in at the Leasing Office or designated building contact.</li><li>Obtain access to the LCP/MDF.</li><li>Contact the assigned Gigstreem Engineer before beginning work.</li><li>Locate the Edge-Router, Gateway, UPS, EBM, PDU, and MDF Aggregation Switch.</li><li>Inspect the network rack and identify the existing primary and redundant circuit connections.</li><li>Take clear photos of the rack, equipment, and existing cabling before making changes.</li></ul>2. Redundant Circuit Patch-In<p>The technician must verify the following circuit assignments:</p><p>CircuitRequired Edge-Router Port<strong>Primary Circuit</strong>SFP+1<strong>Redundant Circuit</strong>SFP+2</p><p><strong>Technician Tasks:</strong></p><ol><li>Identify the existing primary circuit and verify that it is connected to <strong>SFP+1</strong> on the Edge-Router.</li><li>Identify the redundant circuit handoff and associated patch cable.</li><li>Verify the required optic, connector, and patch cable type with Engineering.</li><li>Patch the redundant circuit into <strong>SFP+2</strong> on the Edge-Router.</li><li>Verify that the fiber or Ethernet connection is properly seated and secured, as applicable.</li><li>Connect the Windows laptop to the MikroTik Edge-Router using the appropriate console connection if requested.</li><li>Work with the assigned Engineer to confirm interface status and circuit connectivity.</li><li>Assist Engineering with any additional physical troubleshooting required.</li></ol><p><strong>Do not disconnect or interrupt the existing primary circuit without explicit Engineering authorization.</strong></p>3. Circuit Turn-Up (TTU) &amp; Validation<p>Working remotely with the assigned Gigstreem Engineer:</p><ul><li>Confirm the redundant circuit is physically connected to the correct port.</li><li>Verify the Edge-Router detects the redundant circuit.</li><li>Assist Engineering with circuit provisioning and activation.</li><li>Verify interface status, link stability, and connectivity.</li><li>Support Engineering with any required circuit testing.</li><li>Confirm the primary circuit remains operational.</li><li>Assist Engineering with redundancy/failover validation only if specifically authorized and confirmed non-service-affecting.</li><li>Document the final circuit connection and test results.</li><li>Obtain Engineering confirmation that the redundant circuit TTU is complete.</li></ul>4. UPS, EBM &amp; Rack Inspection<p>Perform the following visual inspections according to the approved rack elevation and design diagram.</p><p><strong>General Rack &amp; UPS Inspection</strong></p><ol><li>Verify the LCP/MDF is built according to the approved rack elevation.</li><li>Confirm the UPS battery is properly connected behind the front faceplate.</li><li>Inspect the EBM and verify that the umbilical and data cords are connected.</li><li>Verify that the UPS is connected to <strong>Gateway eth7</strong>.</li></ol><p><strong>Edge-Router Power Connections</strong></p><ol><li>Verify Edge-Router <strong>PS-A</strong> is labeled at both ends and connected to the designated <strong>PDU</strong> outlet.</li><li>Verify Edge-Router <strong>PS-B</strong> is labeled at both ends and connected to <strong>UPS-critical</strong>, according to the design diagram.</li></ol><p><strong>Gateway Power Connections</strong></p><ol><li>Verify Gateway <strong>PS-A</strong> is labeled at both ends and connected to the designated <strong>PDU</strong> outlet.</li><li>Verify Gateway <strong>PS-B</strong> is labeled at both ends and connected to <strong>UPS-critical</strong>, according to the design diagram.</li></ol><p><strong>MDF Aggregation Switch Power Connections</strong></p><ol><li>Verify MDF Aggregation Switch <strong>PS-A</strong> is labeled at both ends and connected to the designated <strong>PDU</strong> outlet.</li><li>Verify MDF Aggregation Switch <strong>PS-B</strong> is labeled at both ends and connected to <strong>UPS-critical</strong>, according to the design diagram.</li></ol><p><strong>Provider Equipment Power Connections</strong></p><ol><li>Verify Provider 1 <strong>PS-A</strong> is labeled at both ends and connected to the designated <strong>PDU</strong> outlet.</li><li>Verify Provider 1 <strong>PS-B</strong> is labeled at both ends and connected to <strong>UPS-noncritical</strong>, according to the design diagram.</li><li>Inspect any additional provider equipment identified in the approved design and verify its PS-A connection and labeling.</li><li>Inspect any additional provider equipment identified in the approved design and verify its PS-B connection and labeling.</li></ol><p><strong>Important:</strong> The dispatch request repeats Provider 1 for steps 13–14. Engineering must confirm whether these steps refer to a second provider before the technician makes any changes.</p><p>Document any missing labels, incorrect connections, disconnected cables, or deviations from the approved rack elevation.</p>5. UPS Failover &amp; Power Validation<p>The requested power validation includes the following procedures.</p><p><strong>Step 15 – UPS Battery Failover Test</strong></p><ul><li>Coordinate with the assigned NetOps Engineer before testing.</li><li>Verify the UPS and EBM are connected and reporting normally.</li><li>Confirm that an approved procedure and authorization are in place.</li><li>If explicitly authorized, perform a controlled commercial-power interruption using the approved method.</li><li>Verify with NetOps that the UPS carries the connected load without tripping breakers.</li><li>Confirm that commercial power is restored and all equipment remains operational.</li></ul><p><strong>Step 16 – Power Supply Verification</strong></p><p><strong>The assigned NetOps Engineer will remotely access the UPS/PDU and validate the labeled power supply connections.</strong></p><ul><li>NetOps will identify the power supply to be tested.</li><li>NetOps will remotely turn off one designated power supply at a time.</li><li>Confirm that the correct power supply responds according to its label.</li><li>Restore that power supply before proceeding to the next.</li><li>Document incorrect labels, unexpected power behavior, or equipment alarms.</li></ul><p><strong>Critical Safety Requirement:</strong> This work order is marked <strong>non-service-affecting</strong>. Do not interrupt commercial power, switch off power supplies, or conduct failover tests unless Engineering has explicitly confirmed that the procedure is safe, authorized, and will not affect service. If a service interruption is possible, defer the test until an approved maintenance window is arranged.</p>6. Final Verification &amp; Documentation<p>Before leaving the property:</p><ul><li>Confirm the primary circuit is connected to <strong>SFP+1</strong>.</li><li>Confirm the redundant circuit is connected to <strong>SFP+2</strong>.</li><li>Obtain Engineering confirmation that the redundant circuit is online and operational.</li><li>Document circuit testing and TTU results.</li><li>Complete the rack, UPS, EBM, and power connection inspections.</li><li>Record any labeling or wiring discrepancies.</li><li>Document whether UPS failover and power supply validation were completed or deferred.</li><li>Take clear photos of:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Edge-Router and SFP+ ports.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Primary and redundant circuit connections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Complete network rack.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">UPS and EBM connections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gateway eth7 connection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">PDU and power supply labels.</li><li>Provide detailed completion notes.</li><li>Do not leave until the assigned Engineer confirms that the onsite work is complete.</li></ul>Technician Expectations<ul><li><strong>Contact the onsite customer/property contact at least 30 minutes before arrival</strong> with an accurate ETA.</li><li>Check in with property management and present Field Nation ID and credentials.</li><li>Maintain communication with the assigned Gigstreem Engineer throughout the dispatch.</li><li>Arrive fully prepared with the required laptop, software, console cable, and network tools.</li><li>Represent Gigstreem professionally.</li><li>Do not share unnecessary internal or technical details with property staff.</li><li>Do not make unauthorized configuration changes.</li><li>Protect existing network services throughout the work.</li><li>Submit complete notes and photographs.</li></ul><p>⚠️ <em>If the technician arrives unprepared, demonstrates poor presentation, or performs substandard work, Gigstreem reserves the right to withhold payment for the work order.</em></p>Required Experience<p>The technician MUST have experience with:</p><ul><li><strong>MikroTik Edge-Router troubleshooting and console connectivity.</strong></li><li><strong>Winbox and PuTTY.</strong></li><li><strong>Primary and redundant circuit patch-in.</strong></li><li><strong>SFP/SFP+ interfaces and network uplinks.</strong></li><li><strong>Circuit turn-up and remote Engineering coordination.</strong></li><li><strong>Layer 1 and Layer 2 network troubleshooting.</strong></li><li><strong>UPS, EBM, and PDU inspection.</strong></li><li><strong>Redundant power supply identification and labeling.</strong></li><li><strong>Working safely around active network infrastructure.</strong></li><li><strong>MDF/LCP telecommunications environments.</strong></li></ul>Required Tools – Mandatory<p>The technician must arrive with:</p><ul><li><strong>Windows laptop with Winbox and PuTTY installed.</strong></li><li><strong>RJ45-to-USB console cable compatible with MikroTik equipment.</strong></li><li><strong>Standard RJ45 Ethernet cable.</strong></li><li><strong>Laptop charger.</strong></li><li><strong>Mobile hotspot.</strong></li><li><strong>USB-C-to-USB-C console cable.</strong></li><li><strong>Known-good Ethernet patch cables.</strong></li><li><strong>Appropriate network hand tools.</strong></li><li><strong>Screwdriver set.</strong></li><li><strong>Flashlight/headlamp.</strong></li><li><strong>Phone with working camera.</strong></li><li><strong>Cable labels and marker.</strong></li><li><strong>Appropriate PPE.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Note:</strong> The technician must confirm any additional fiber patch cables, optical transceivers, or adapters required for the redundant circuit with Engineering before the visit.</p>","markdown":"**Gigstreem is a national Internet service provider delivering high-quality internet services to residential and commercial properties across the country.**\n\nWork Order Details**Property:** **Address:** 7920 Oak Estate St, Raleigh, NC 27617\n\n**Customer:** Cortland\n\n**Work Location:** LCP/MDF – Main Network Equipment Room\n\nScope of Work (SOW)Dispatch an experienced Field Nation network technician to **Cortland Brier Creek** to complete the redundant circuit patch-in and Turn-Up (TTU), working directly with an assigned Gigstreem Engineer remotely.\n\nThe technician must physically connect the redundant circuit to the designated Edge-Router port, assist Engineering with circuit activation and testing, and complete the required visual power provisioning inspections.\n\n**IMPORTANT: The technician MUST arrive with a Windows laptop with Winbox and PuTTY installed, an RJ45 Ethernet cable, and an RJ45-to-USB console cable compatible with MikroTik equipment.**\n\n1. Site Access & Initial Inspection- Contact the onsite property contact at least 30 minutes before arrival to provide an accurate ETA.\n- Check in at the Leasing Office or designated building contact.\n- Obtain access to the LCP/MDF.\n- Contact the assigned Gigstreem Engineer before beginning work.\n- Locate the Edge-Router, Gateway, UPS, EBM, PDU, and MDF Aggregation Switch.\n- Inspect the network rack and identify the existing primary and redundant circuit connections.\n- Take clear photos of the rack, equipment, and existing cabling before making changes.\n\n2. Redundant Circuit Patch-InThe technician must verify the following circuit assignments:\n\nCircuitRequired Edge-Router Port**Primary Circuit**SFP+1**Redundant Circuit**SFP+2\n\n**Technician Tasks:**\n\n1. Identify the existing primary circuit and verify that it is connected to **SFP+1** on the Edge-Router.\n2. Identify the redundant circuit handoff and associated patch cable.\n3. Verify the required optic, connector, and patch cable type with Engineering.\n4. Patch the redundant circuit into **SFP+2** on the Edge-Router.\n5. Verify that the fiber or Ethernet connection is properly seated and secured, as applicable.\n6. Connect the Windows laptop to the MikroTik Edge-Router using the appropriate console connection if requested.\n7. Work with the assigned Engineer to confirm interface status and circuit connectivity.\n8. Assist Engineering with any additional physical troubleshooting required.\n\n**Do not disconnect or interrupt the existing primary circuit without explicit Engineering authorization.**\n\n3. Circuit Turn-Up (TTU) & ValidationWorking remotely with the assigned Gigstreem Engineer:\n\n- Confirm the redundant circuit is physically connected to the correct port.\n- Verify the Edge-Router detects the redundant circuit.\n- Assist Engineering with circuit provisioning and activation.\n- Verify interface status, link stability, and connectivity.\n- Support Engineering with any required circuit testing.\n- Confirm the primary circuit remains operational.\n- Assist Engineering with redundancy/failover validation only if specifically authorized and confirmed non-service-affecting.\n- Document the final circuit connection and test results.\n- Obtain Engineering confirmation that the redundant circuit TTU is complete.\n\n4. UPS, EBM & Rack InspectionPerform the following visual inspections according to the approved rack elevation and design diagram.\n\n**General Rack & UPS Inspection**\n\n1. Verify the LCP/MDF is built according to the approved rack elevation.\n2. Confirm the UPS battery is properly connected behind the front faceplate.\n3. Inspect the EBM and verify that the umbilical and data cords are connected.\n4. Verify that the UPS is connected to **Gateway eth7**.\n\n**Edge-Router Power Connections**\n\n1. Verify Edge-Router **PS-A** is labeled at both ends and connected to the designated **PDU** outlet.\n2. Verify Edge-Router **PS-B** is labeled at both ends and connected to **UPS-critical**, according to the design diagram.\n\n**Gateway Power Connections**\n\n1. Verify Gateway **PS-A** is labeled at both ends and connected to the designated **PDU** outlet.\n2. Verify Gateway **PS-B** is labeled at both ends and connected to **UPS-critical**, according to the design diagram.\n\n**MDF Aggregation Switch Power Connections**\n\n1. Verify MDF Aggregation Switch **PS-A** is labeled at both ends and connected to the designated **PDU** outlet.\n2. Verify MDF Aggregation Switch **PS-B** is labeled at both ends and connected to **UPS-critical**, according to the design diagram.\n\n**Provider Equipment Power Connections**\n\n1. Verify Provider 1 **PS-A** is labeled at both ends and connected to the designated **PDU** outlet.\n2. Verify Provider 1 **PS-B** is labeled at both ends and connected to **UPS-noncritical**, according to the design diagram.\n3. Inspect any additional provider equipment identified in the approved design and verify its PS-A connection and labeling.\n4. Inspect any additional provider equipment identified in the approved design and verify its PS-B connection and labeling.\n\n**Important:** The dispatch request repeats Provider 1 for steps 13–14. Engineering must confirm whether these steps refer to a second provider before the technician makes any changes.\n\nDocument any missing labels, incorrect connections, disconnected cables, or deviations from the approved rack elevation.\n\n5. UPS Failover & Power ValidationThe requested power validation includes the following procedures.\n\n**Step 15 – UPS Battery Failover Test**\n\n- Coordinate with the assigned NetOps Engineer before testing.\n- Verify the UPS and EBM are connected and reporting normally.\n- Confirm that an approved procedure and authorization are in place.\n- If explicitly authorized, perform a controlled commercial-power interruption using the approved method.\n- Verify with NetOps that the UPS carries the connected load without tripping breakers.\n- Confirm that commercial power is restored and all equipment remains operational.\n\n**Step 16 – Power Supply Verification**\n\n**The assigned NetOps Engineer will remotely access the UPS/PDU and validate the labeled power supply connections.**\n\n- NetOps will identify the power supply to be tested.\n- NetOps will remotely turn off one designated power supply at a time.\n- Confirm that the correct power supply responds according to its label.\n- Restore that power supply before proceeding to the next.\n- Document incorrect labels, unexpected power behavior, or equipment alarms.\n\n**Critical Safety Requirement:** This work order is marked **non-service-affecting**. Do not interrupt commercial power, switch off power supplies, or conduct failover tests unless Engineering has explicitly confirmed that the procedure is safe, authorized, and will not affect service. If a service interruption is possible, defer the test until an approved maintenance window is arranged.\n\n6. Final Verification & DocumentationBefore leaving the property:\n\n- Confirm the primary circuit is connected to **SFP+1**.\n- Confirm the redundant circuit is connected to **SFP+2**.\n- Obtain Engineering confirmation that the redundant circuit is online and operational.\n- Document circuit testing and TTU results.\n- Complete the rack, UPS, EBM, and power connection inspections.\n- Record any labeling or wiring discrepancies.\n- Document whether UPS failover and power supply validation were completed or deferred.\n- Take clear photos of:\n- Edge-Router and SFP+ ports.\n- Primary and redundant circuit connections.\n- Complete network rack.\n- UPS and EBM connections.\n- Gateway eth7 connection.\n- PDU and power supply labels.\n- Provide detailed completion notes.\n- Do not leave until the assigned Engineer confirms that the onsite work is complete.\n\nTechnician Expectations- **Contact the onsite customer/property contact at least 30 minutes before arrival** with an accurate ETA.\n- Check in with property management and present Field Nation ID and credentials.\n- Maintain communication with the assigned Gigstreem Engineer throughout the dispatch.\n- Arrive fully prepared with the required laptop, software, console cable, and network tools.\n- Represent Gigstreem professionally.\n- Do not share unnecessary internal or technical details with property staff.\n- Do not make unauthorized configuration changes.\n- Protect existing network services throughout the work.\n- Submit complete notes and photographs.\n\n⚠️ *If the technician arrives unprepared, demonstrates poor presentation, or performs substandard work, Gigstreem reserves the right to withhold payment for the work order.*\n\nRequired ExperienceThe technician MUST have experience with:\n\n- **MikroTik Edge-Router troubleshooting and console connectivity.**\n- **Winbox and PuTTY.**\n- **Primary and redundant circuit patch-in.**\n- **SFP/SFP+ interfaces and network uplinks.**\n- **Circuit turn-up and remote Engineering coordination.**\n- **Layer 1 and Layer 2 network troubleshooting.**\n- **UPS, EBM, and PDU inspection.**\n- **Redundant power supply identification and labeling.**\n- **Working safely around active network infrastructure.**\n- **MDF/LCP telecommunications environments.**\n\nRequired Tools – MandatoryThe technician must arrive with:\n\n- **Windows laptop with Winbox and PuTTY installed.**\n- **RJ45-to-USB console cable compatible with MikroTik equipment.**\n- **Standard RJ45 Ethernet cable.**\n- **Laptop charger.**\n- **Mobile hotspot.**\n- **USB-C-to-USB-C console cable.**\n- **Known-good Ethernet patch cables.**\n- **Appropriate network hand tools.**\n- **Screwdriver set.**\n- **Flashlight/headlamp.**\n- **Phone with working camera.**\n- **Cable labels and marker.**\n- **Appropriate PPE.**\n\n**Note:** The technician must confirm any additional fiber patch cables, optical transceivers, or adapters required for the redundant circuit with Engineering before the visit.","actions":[]},"isRequestedWindow":false,"time":{"start":"2026-10-22T12:00","end":"2026-10-22T18:00"},"payRange":{"min":300,"max":525},"payType":"blended","payStructure":{"type":"blended","base":{"units":3,"amount":300},