{"job":{"id":20336253,"platform":"FieldNation","company":"StrikeCheck","title":"Express Damage Assessment - StrikeCheck Appointment A-2752236","description":{"html":"<p>*Please make sure to read through all instructions to avoid a second visit to the property and/or non-payment*</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The following items need to be <strong><em>documented</em></strong>, <strong><em>inspected</em></strong>, <strong><em>tested</em></strong> and <strong><em>photographed, even if they are found to be non-damaged</em></strong>:</p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"color: rgb(32, 33, 36);\"><b><ul><li>Washer/Dryer Combination : Brand Unknown : 1</li></ul></b></span></p><p><br></p><p><strong>ATTENTION: Every technician must use the CCG IQ Inspection Wizard to submit their findings. We cannot complete our payment to you if the findings are submitted to the Field Nation platform or via email. All findings, including non-damaged items on your list, must be sent directly to StrikeCheck using our CCG IQ Inspection Wizard via our Android app or web portal. If any item cannot be documented you must include a statement in your notes explaining those circumstances.</strong></p><p><strong>If you are unsure of how to use our Android app or web portal, or are having issues while you are onsite, please call (888) 980-8544 and a live representative will talk you through the submission process. Android users will need to download the \"CCG IQ Inspection Wizard\" in the app store on your device. Our office hours are 8am-8pm EST Monday-Friday and 8am-5pm on Saturdays. If no one is available to take your call please leave a message and we will return your call ASAP!</strong></p><p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>Testing and Documentation Requirements:</strong></p><ol><li>Full testing and documentation procedures on a per-item type basis can be found under the “Resources” tab in our web app. Select “StrikeCheck” in the “Libraries” folder to review the requirements before every inspection to ensure all required data is being captured.</li><li>Basic continuity/ohm testing is required using a digital multimeter. Verify a functional power source or outlet with your meter and document those readings. If needed, reset any associated circuit breakers but do not otherwise alter the electrical panel.&nbsp;</li><li>Apply power and operationally test ALL FUNCTIONS of every item. Notate any connected items (speakers to receiver, cameras to DVR, etc.) and advise if further testing is necessary due to the failure of one of those components (\"Can't test speakers due to damaged receiver\").&nbsp;</li><li>Document and photograph all obtainable readings/testing. <strong>If the item is not damaged or has already been replaced with new equipment, we still need the same amount of evidence (photos and test results) to support your findings.</strong> If the item is not operating properly, determine the causation of damage.&nbsp;</li><li>In addition to photos of the loss location and your meter readings, we require that you provide a minimum of 3 pictures per item. These pictures should illustrate where/how the item is installed, the overall condition of the item, any visual damages, and <strong><em>most importantly, </em></strong>the data plate. Also photograph any associated components connected to the item you are inspecting. Additional photographs and documentation are always encouraged.</li></ol><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Inspection Requirements:</strong></p><ul><li>A smart phone with access to our Android app or web app portal for Apple iOS users is the preferred method to document and upload your findings in real time. Access the app or portal before the inspection begins and it will walk you through all steps required for each item you enter.</li><li>Use of a camera and our Technician Worksheets is an alternate option that will require a computer to access the web app portal in order to upload photos as well as transcribing your detailed technical documentation from the worksheets into our system.</li><li>Multimeter/VOM and a basic toolkit</li><li>Must be professionally dressed (wear a clean collared shirt and pants; No shorts, no t-shirts, no flip-flops, and no ripped jeans). Avoid wearing anything that will identify your personal employer/company name or contact info to prevent insureds from calling you directly after you complete your assignment. This is an independent inspection on behalf of StrikeCheck, an insurance consulting company. <strong>You</strong> <strong>must represent yourself as being with StrikeCheck</strong> <strong>when you arrive onsite</strong>.</li><li>Must be willing to display contractor photo identification (if issued) and must have appropriate background and skills to perform the listed work order requirements</li><li>Must have passed a background check and drug test.&nbsp;</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Submission Requirements:</strong></p><p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p><ul><li>iPhone/iPad users: Upload required information via our <strong>web portal</strong> the same day as the inspection using a secure password. Click the \"Login\" link below or use this web address to access the portal:&nbsp;https://consolidatedclaims.my.site.com/ccginspector/&nbsp;</li><li>All findings, pictures, and determinations should be submitted on the same day as the inspection. <strong>Your deadline to submit is 24 hours from the scheduled appointment start time. Failure to do so may result in non-payment and could affect your rating.</strong></li><li>Your work order is not considered complete and payable until findings are submitted through the Android app or web portal and is approved by our Processing Department. Please make yourself available for follow-up calls if we have any questions regarding your findings.</li></ul><p><strong style=\"color: rgb(34, 34, 34);\">Scheduling Requirements:</strong></p><ul><li>These work orders most likely take place in a residential property, and therefore requires the ETA to be approved by the client.</li><li>When you are assigned this work order, please submit an ETA as soon as possible.</li><li>We will reach out to the client to confirm that they are able to be onsite during your proposed ETA. <strong><u>The appointment is not considered scheduled until we follow up with you to confirm the proposed time works for all parties.</u></strong></li><li>A project manager will message you via the Field Nation App when the ETA has been confirmed with the insured. If you go to the loss location before we confirm the appointment is set, you will not be afforded a trip fee in the event that the insured is not present when you arrive.</li><li><strong>You must update our project managers and dispatch coordinators regularly on work order progress, any service delays, or schedule conflicts. If you cannot make an appointment CALL OUR OFFICE IMMEDIATELY TO RESCHEDULE. Field Nation messages are not an accepted medium for rescheduling or canceling appointments!</strong></li><li>If you go to the property, but the client does not show for the appointment, call us immediately so we can follow up with the client and determine if they will be arriving soon. <strong>If the client misses the appointment, you must take a clear photo of the house/property, showing the address so we can confirm you were there.</strong></li><li>StrikeCheck will provide a 50% trip fee to you up to $100 if the insured cannot make the appointment.&nbsp;</li><li>StrikeCheck will provide a 50% trip fee to you up to $100 if the scope of work cannot be completed for any reason</li><li>StrikeCheck will <em>not </em>provide a trip fee if you arrive at an appointment that has <em>not </em>been confirmed with the client and the project manager. <strong><u>The appointment is not considered scheduled until we follow up with you to confirm the proposed time works for all parties.</u></strong></li></ul>","markdown":"\\*Please make sure to read through all instructions to avoid a second visit to the property and/or non-payment\\*\n\nThe following items need to be ***documented***, ***inspected***, ***tested*** and ***photographed, even if they are found to be non-damaged***:\n\n**- Washer/Dryer Combination : Brand Unknown : 1**\n\n**ATTENTION: Every technician must use the CCG IQ Inspection Wizard to submit their findings. We cannot complete our payment to you if the findings are submitted to the Field Nation platform or via email. All findings, including non-damaged items on your list, must be sent directly to StrikeCheck using our CCG IQ Inspection Wizard via our Android app or web portal. If any item cannot be documented you must include a statement in your notes explaining those circumstances.**\n\n**If you are unsure of how to use our Android app or web portal, or are having issues while you are onsite, please call (888) 980-8544 and a live representative will talk you through the submission process. Android users will need to download the \"CCG IQ Inspection Wizard\" in the app store on your device. Our office hours are 8am-8pm EST Monday-Friday and 8am-5pm on Saturdays. If no one is available to take your call please leave a message and we will return your call ASAP!**\n\n**Testing and Documentation Requirements:**\n\n1. Full testing and documentation procedures on a per-item type basis can be found under the “Resources” tab in our web app. Select “StrikeCheck” in the “Libraries” folder to review the requirements before every inspection to ensure all required data is being captured.\n2. Basic continuity/ohm testing is required using a digital multimeter. Verify a functional power source or outlet with your meter and document those readings. If needed, reset any associated circuit breakers but do not otherwise alter the electrical panel.\n3. Apply power and operationally test ALL FUNCTIONS of every item. Notate any connected items (speakers to receiver, cameras to DVR, etc.) and advise if further testing is necessary due to the failure of one of those components (\"Can't test speakers due to damaged receiver\").\n4. Document and photograph all obtainable readings/testing. **If the item is not damaged or has already been replaced with new equipment, we still need the same amount of evidence (photos and test results) to support your findings.** If the item is not operating properly, determine the causation of damage.\n5. In addition to photos of the loss location and your meter readings, we require that you provide a minimum of 3 pictures per item. These pictures should illustrate where/how the item is installed, the overall condition of the item, any visual damages, and ***most importantly,*** the data plate. Also photograph any associated components connected to the item you are inspecting. Additional photographs and documentation are always encouraged.\n\n**Inspection Requirements:**\n\n- A smart phone with access to our Android app or web app portal for Apple iOS users is the preferred method to document and upload your findings in real time. Access the app or portal before the inspection begins and it will walk you through all steps required for each item you enter.\n- Use of a camera and our Technician Worksheets is an alternate option that will require a computer to access the web app portal in order to upload photos as well as transcribing your detailed technical documentation from the worksheets into our system.\n- Multimeter/VOM and a basic toolkit\n- Must be professionally dressed (wear a clean collared shirt and pants; No shorts, no t-shirts, no flip-flops, and no ripped jeans). Avoid wearing anything that will identify your personal employer/company name or contact info to prevent insureds from calling you directly after you complete your assignment. This is an independent inspection on behalf of StrikeCheck, an insurance consulting company. **You** **must represent yourself as being with StrikeCheck** **when you arrive onsite**.\n- Must be willing to display contractor photo identification (if issued) and must have appropriate background and skills to perform the listed work order requirements\n- Must have passed a background check and drug test.\n\n**Submission Requirements:**\n\n- iPhone/iPad users: Upload required information via our **web portal** the same day as the inspection using a secure password. Click the \"Login\" link below or use this web address to access the portal: https://consolidatedclaims.my.site.com/ccginspector/&nbsp;\n- All findings, pictures, and determinations should be submitted on the same day as the inspection. **Your deadline to submit is 24 hours from the scheduled appointment start time. Failure to do so may result in non-payment and could affect your rating.**\n- Your work order is not considered complete and payable until findings are submitted through the Android app or web portal and is approved by our Processing Department. Please make yourself available for follow-up calls if we have any questions regarding your findings.\n\n**Scheduling Requirements:**\n\n- These work orders most likely take place in a residential property, and therefore requires the ETA to be approved by the client.\n- When you are assigned this work order, please submit an ETA as soon as possible.\n- We will reach out to the client to confirm that they are able to be onsite during your proposed ETA. **The appointment is not considered scheduled until we follow up with you to confirm the proposed time works for all parties.**\n- A project manager will message you via the Field Nation App when the ETA has been confirmed with the insured. If you go to the loss location before we confirm the appointment is set, you will not be afforded a trip fee in the event that the insured is not present when you arrive.\n- **You must update our project managers and dispatch coordinators regularly on work order progress, any service delays, or schedule conflicts. If you cannot make an appointment CALL OUR OFFICE IMMEDIATELY TO RESCHEDULE. Field Nation messages are not an accepted medium for rescheduling or canceling appointments!**\n- If you go to the property, but the client does not show for the appointment, call us immediately so we can follow up with the client and determine if they will be arriving soon. **If the client misses the appointment, you must take a clear photo of the house/property, showing the address so we can confirm you were there.**\n- StrikeCheck will provide a 50% trip fee to you up to $100 if the insured cannot make the appointment.\n- StrikeCheck will provide a 50% trip fee to you up to $100 if the scope of work cannot be completed for any reason\n- StrikeCheck will *not* provide a trip fee if you arrive at an appointment that has *not* been confirmed with the client and the project manager. **The appointment is not considered scheduled until we follow up with you to confirm the proposed time works for all parties.**","actions":[]},"isRequestedWindow":true,"time":{"start":"2026-11-09T07:00","end":"2026-11-09T09:00","latestStart":"2026-11-09T19:00"},"payRange":{"min":100,"max":100},"payType":"fixed","payStructure":null,"hourlyRate":0,"estLaborHours":2,"distance":46.20118873227113},"result":{"eligible":false,"counterOffer":null,"reason":"BLOCKED_KEYWORD","rejectDetails":"Blocked keyword \"display\" in description"},"availableBlocks":[{"start":"2026-11-09T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-09T15:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-09T20:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-10T01:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-10T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-10T15:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-10T18:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-10T21:30:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-11T00:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-11T01:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-12T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T14:30:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-12T23:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-13T01:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-13T20:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-14T01:00:00.000Z"}],"busyBlocks":[{"start":"2026-11-09T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-09T16:30:00.000Z","summary":"Survey FN"},{"start":"2026-11-10T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-10T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"Granite"},{"start":"2026-11-12T14:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T23:30:00.000Z","summary":"Progitech"},{"start":"2026-11-09T17:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-09T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"Gap"},{"start":"2026-11-12T22:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T23:00:00.000Z","summary":"Pay bills BoA + Amex"},{"start":"2026-11-11T05:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T05:00:00.000Z","summary":"Veterans Day"},{"start":"2026-11-10T21:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-11T00:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 ***Thermo Fisher | Network Cutover Assistance*** (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-17T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 The Preiss Company-05974911-Epik Cutover & Test (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-10T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-10T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 05636592 - 1013 Classic Rd | VTM CUT (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-16T21:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T00:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 Advance Auto Parts - Phone Swap - 4810 Raleigh  NC (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-17T17:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T20:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 Rollins/Orkin Pest Control - FortiGate Swap & edgebootPro installation - Raleigh, NC (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-16T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-16T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"[INC#: WO 2026 Nov 16 SITE#: RTP/DC3]   To Be Updated"},{"start":"2026-11-13T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-13T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"[INC#: 00740402 - ATT SITE#: RTP/DC3]   To Be Updated"},{"start":"2026-11-11T13:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-11T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"POS equipment install"},{"start":"2026-11-09T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-09T17:00:00.000Z","summary":"Dream Finders - Site Survey - Networking"}],"action":"SKIP","ts":1794265018361}
{"job":{"id":20336238,"platform":"FieldNation","company":"Splice Communications","title":"Site survey for Cable Run and APs installation","description":{"html":"<p><strong>PLEASE REPRESENT YOURSELF AS A SPLICE TECHNICIAN WHEN SPEAKING WITH ONSITE CONTACTS OR WITH CUSTOMER TECHNICAL CONTACTS.</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>﻿Mandatory Scope Discussion</strong></p>\n<p>Technicians who have <strong>not previously completed work for this specific project phase</strong> must participate in a pre-visit scope discussion.</p>\n<p>If contacted, please plan to attend. <strong>Failure to complete the required discussion may result in removal from the work order.</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong><u>SCOPE OF WORK SUMMARY:</u></strong></p>\n<p>PLEASE REPRESENT YOURSELF AS A SPLICE TECHNICIAN WHEN SPEAKING WITH ONSITE CONTACTS OR CUSTOMER TECHNICAL CONTACTS.</p>\n<p>Technician need to perform an onsite survey at designated location to evaluate the requirements for upcoming structured cabling and Wireless Access Point (AP) installation.</p>\n<p>Fiber-optic installation has already been completed. The purpose of this visit is to assess the remaining installation requirements, including cable pathways, AP mounting locations, existing infrastructure, required materials, and anticipated labor hours. (This is a survey-only visit. No cabling installation, AP mounting, or configuration work is authorized.)</p>\n<p>The technician will be provided with floor plans identifying the proposed AP locations. These floor plans must be reviewed and compared against actual site conditions.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>1. ONSITE CHECK-IN &amp; INITIAL REVIEW</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>• Check in with the Manager on Duty (MOD) or designated onsite contact.</p>\n<p>• Introduce yourself as a Splice technician and explain the purpose of the visit.</p>\n<p>• Review the provided floor plans and identify the designated AP locations.</p>\n<p>• Confirm access to all relevant areas, including IDF/MDF rooms, ceilings, and other spaces required to evaluate cable pathways.</p>\n<p>• Confirm with the onsite contact whether AP equipment and installation materials have already been delivered or are available onsite.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>2. EQUIPMENT &amp; EXISTING INFRASTRUCTURE VERIFICATION</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>• Verify whether the Wireless Access Points identified in the floor plans are physically available onsite.</p>\n<p>• If APs are onsite, document:</p>\n<p>o Quantity and equipment models.</p>\n<p>o Manufacturer, if identifiable.</p>\n<p>o Equipment condition.</p>\n<p>o Whether mounting hardware and accessories are available.</p>\n<p>• If APs are not onsite, document this and confirm whether the onsite contact has any information regarding equipment delivery or availability.</p>\n<p>• Identify any existing network racks, switches, patch panels, network cabling, and available infrastructure relevant to the proposed installation.</p>\n<p>• Verify the location and accessibility of the previously installed fiber-optic connection.</p>\n<p>• Document any additional infrastructure or equipment that may be required for the installation.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>3. STRUCTURED CABLING SITE SURVEY</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>• Review the floor plans and identify the proposed cable termination points and AP locations.</p>\n<p>• Evaluate potential cable pathways from the network equipment room (MDF/IDF) to each designated AP location.</p>\n<p>• Inspect accessible ceiling spaces, conduits, cable trays, pathways, and other potential routing options.</p>\n<p>• Determine whether existing pathways can be reused or if new pathways will be required.</p>\n<p>• Identify potential obstacles or installation challenges, including:</p>\n<p>o Inaccessible or restricted ceiling spaces.</p>\n<p>o Walls or structures requiring penetration.</p>\n<p>o Long cable routes or difficult access areas.</p>\n<p>o Areas requiring lifts, ladders, or special equipment.</p>\n<p>• Estimate the approximate cable length required for each AP location.</p>\n<p>• Identify the proposed cable type and any additional materials required.</p>\n<p>• Document the proposed cable route and termination requirements for each location.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>4. WIRELESS ACCESS POINT (AP) MOUNTING SURVEY</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>• Locate and verify each AP installation point identified on the provided floor plans.</p>\n<p>• Compare the proposed locations against actual site conditions.</p>\n<p>• Evaluate ceiling or wall mounting conditions and determine the appropriate mounting method.</p>\n<p>• Verify ceiling type, mounting surface, approximate installation height, and accessibility.</p>\n<p>• Identify whether existing mounting hardware can be reused or if new brackets or accessories will be required.</p>\n<p>• Determine whether any locations require special equipment, additional labor, or alternative mounting solutions.</p>\n<p>• Document any discrepancies between the floor plans and actual site conditions.</p>\n<p>• If a proposed AP location is not suitable, recommend an alternative location and explain the reason.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>5. LABOR, MATERIALS &amp; INSTALLATION REQUIREMENTS</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>Based on the site inspection, provide an estimated breakdown of the work required for the future installation.</p>\n<p>• Estimate the number of new cable runs required.</p>\n<p>• Estimate the approximate cable length for each run.</p>\n<p>• Identify required materials, including:</p>\n<p>o Ethernet cabling.</p>\n<p>o Keystone jacks, patch panels, and termination hardware, if needed.</p>\n<p>o Mounting brackets and AP accessories.</p>\n<p>o Conduit, cable trays, or other pathway materials, if required.</p>\n<p>o Cable management and labeling materials.</p>\n<p>• Identify any special tools or equipment required.</p>\n<p>• Estimate the labor hours required for the cabling and AP installation.</p>\n<p>• Identify any access restrictions, scheduling considerations, or dependencies that could affect the installation.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>6. PHOTOS &amp; DOCUMENTATION</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>The technician must provide a complete survey report for each location, including:</p>\n<p>• Photos of the MDF/IDF rooms, network racks, and relevant existing infrastructure.</p>\n<p>• Photos of the previously installed fiber-optic connection and its termination point.</p>\n<p>• Photos of each proposed AP mounting location.</p>\n<p>• Photos of potential cable pathways, ceiling spaces, and any obstacles.</p>\n<p>• Photos of AP equipment and accessories available onsite, if applicable.</p>\n<p>• Annotated floor plans identifying:</p>\n<p>o Proposed AP locations.</p>\n<p>o Proposed cable routes.</p>\n<p>o Network equipment room locations.</p>\n<p>o Any recommended changes to the proposed installation points.</p>\n<p>• Equipment inventory, including AP models and quantities, if available.</p>\n<p>• Estimated cable lengths and required materials.</p>\n<p>• Estimated labor hours and any special equipment requirements.</p>\n<p>• List of discrepancies, installation challenges, and outstanding items.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>All findings must be clearly organized by location.</p>\n<p>IMPORTANT NOTES</p>\n<p>• This WO is strictly for site survey and information gathering.</p>\n<p>• Do not install, terminate, or modify any cabling.</p>\n<p>• Do not mount, connect, or configure any Wireless Access Points.</p>\n<p>• Do not modify the existing fiber-optic installation.</p>\n<p>• Any discrepancies between the provided floor plans and actual site conditions must be documented.</p>\n<p>• If AP equipment is unavailable onsite, report this clearly. Do not assume the equipment has been delivered.</p>\n<p>• Contact Splice before proceeding if any additional work is requested by the onsite contact.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>JOB DURATION: [4–6 hours estimated; confirm]</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>EXPERIENCE</p>\n<p>• Experience performing structured cabling site surveys.</p>\n<p>• Familiarity with commercial network infrastructure, MDF/IDF rooms, and fiber-optic infrastructure.</p>\n<p>• Experience evaluating cable pathways and Ethernet cable installation requirements.</p>\n<p>• Familiarity with wireless access point installation, mounting methods, and common AP equipment.</p>\n<p>• Ability to interpret floor plans and prepare accurate installation estimates.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>REQUIRED TOOLS</p>\n<p>• Laptop.</p>\n<p>• Digital camera or smartphone for documentation.</p>\n<p>• Measuring tape or laser distance meter.</p>\n<p>• Flashlight.</p>\n<p>• Ladder, if required and permitted onsite.</p>\n<p>• Basic hand tools.</p>\n<p>• Any additional tools required to inspect accessible cable pathways.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p>CONTACTS &amp; ESCALATION</p>\n<p>See \"Confidential Information\" or the \"Contacts\" fields for onsite and Splice points of contact.</p>\n<p>Surveying technician is required to complete the cabling site survey form as an Excel file. Surveying technician is also required to supply a thorough work plan as to what the follow-up work will entail (scope of work, total hours, total materials).<strong>Surveying technician is also required to provide this information within 72 hours of the site visit otherwise we will need to cancel the ticket, and find a new technician.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>PLEASE ONLY REQUEST THIS TICKET IF YOU WILL BE ABLE TO RETURN TO THE SITE FOR THE FOLLOW-UP WORK YOU ARE ESTIMATING FOR.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>REQUIREMENTS FOR A COMPLETED SURVEY:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All tasks are completed as requested (please check with your service manager if you feel something does not apply or are unable to obtain).</li>\n<li>You must complete the cabling survey document AS AN EXCEL FILE.&nbsp;If you take written notes that’s fine, but they must be transferred to the EXCEL FILE as it is required to officially complete that task.</li>\n<li>We will need an itemized breakdown of the TIME AND MATERIALS required for any follow-up work as well as a DETAILED follow-up work plan.&nbsp;This would involve all man hours, itemized materials and costs, as well as tasks/steps to having the job completed properly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><u>JOB DURATION AND REQUIRED EXPERIENCE:</u></strong></p>\n<p>Job duration is expected to be 2-3 hours.</p>\n<p>Technician will need to be proficient with low voltage cabling, cable tracing, toning, crimping, and testing.&nbsp;Will need to be well versed with cable management standards, materials (J-Hooks, wire clips, conduit), certifications, local codes, and other building requirements.&nbsp;Technician will need to understand network device connectivity between routers, firewalls, switches, servers and patch panels/punch downs, wall plates, keystones, and end user devices.&nbsp;Will need to have all tools and materials for testing or making new cables, installing face plates or keystones as required.&nbsp;Will need to be comfortable on ladders and lifts, entering and navigating attics and crawl spaces.</p>\n<p>Technicians will need to be proficient with low voltage cabling as it pertains to fiber cabling, using detection, verification, certification testers.&nbsp;Will also need to have the tools and understand the usage of fault locators, light source, and power meters.&nbsp;Will also need to understand fiber optic assembly tools, splicers (splice or splicing tools), multimode and single mode connections, LC/SC ends and connections.&nbsp;Will need to be well versed with cable management standards, materials (J-Hooks, wire clips, conduit), certifications, local codes, and other building requirements.&nbsp;Technician will need to understand network device connectivity between routers, firewalls, switches, servers and patch panels/punch downs, wall plates, keystones, and end user devices.&nbsp;Will need to be comfortable on ladders and lifts, entering and navigating attics and crawl spaces.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong><u>REQUIRED TOOLS:</u></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Laptop with working power supply, ethernet port and Wi-Fi connectivity</li>\n<li>Excel Software (to complete cabling spreadsheet)</li>\n<li>Standard hand tools (screwdrivers, pliers, snips, etc.)</li>\n<li>Label maker</li>\n<li>Patch Cables</li>\n<li>Cabling tools (crimpers, testers, toners, punch down tools)</li>\n<li>Smartphone</li>\n<li>Hot spot</li>\n<li>Ladder (minimum 4-6 foot A-frame, will need to specify if different)</li>\n<li>Measuring tape</li>\n<li>Measuring Wheel</li>\n<li>Fishing tape</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>As stated in the terms related to this work order, as well as the tasks outlined below, all deliverables and tasks must be uploaded and completed as requested.&nbsp;Please contact your Service Manager PRIOR to departing your location if any questions or issues with a particular deliverable/task.&nbsp;All must be uploaded and marked as WORK DONE no later than 24 hours after departing site.&nbsp;There could be financial penalties if this is not adhered to.</strong></p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong><u>MATERIALS:</u></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>TBD</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong><u>PARTS:</u></strong></p>\n<p>{You could put information or tracking numbers/pick-up locations for equipment that is being sent to a location or held for pick-up.}</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong><u>IMPORTANT CONTACTS AND ESCALATION PATH:</u></strong></p>\n<p>CAN BE FOUND UNDER \"CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION\" OR IN THE \"CONTACTS\" FIELDS.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong><u>TASKS/DELIVERABLES</u></strong></p>\n<p>PLEASE FOLLOW THROUGH THE TASKS AND REQUESTED DELIVERABLES LISTED ON THE PORTAL.&nbsp;CONFIRM WITH YOUR SERVICE MANAGER IF A TASK MAY NOT BE APPLICABLE.</p>\n<p><br /></p>\n<p><strong>DRESS CODE:</strong></p>\n<p>Business casual attire (unless doing any low voltage work involving attic spaces/etc.).&nbsp;This would include a clean shirt with no logos, button down or Polo, khakis or jeans with no holes, dress shoes and/or sneakers with no flashy design.</p>","markdown":"**PLEASE REPRESENT YOURSELF AS A SPLICE TECHNICIAN WHEN SPEAKING WITH ONSITE CONTACTS OR WITH CUSTOMER TECHNICAL CONTACTS.**\n\n**﻿Mandatory Scope Discussion**\n\nTechnicians who have **not previously completed work for this specific project phase** must participate in a pre-visit scope discussion.\n\nIf contacted, please plan to attend. **Failure to complete the required discussion may result in removal from the work order.**\n\n**SCOPE OF WORK SUMMARY:**\n\nPLEASE REPRESENT YOURSELF AS A SPLICE TECHNICIAN WHEN SPEAKING WITH ONSITE CONTACTS OR CUSTOMER TECHNICAL CONTACTS.\n\nTechnician need to perform an onsite survey at designated location to evaluate the requirements for upcoming structured cabling and Wireless Access Point (AP) installation.\n\nFiber-optic installation has already been completed. The purpose of this visit is to assess the remaining installation requirements, including cable pathways, AP mounting locations, existing infrastructure, required materials, and anticipated labor hours. (This is a survey-only visit. No cabling installation, AP mounting, or configuration work is authorized.)\n\nThe technician will be provided with floor plans identifying the proposed AP locations. These floor plans must be reviewed and compared against actual site conditions.\n\n**1. ONSITE CHECK-IN &amp; INITIAL REVIEW**\n\n• Check in with the Manager on Duty (MOD) or designated onsite contact.\n\n• Introduce yourself as a Splice technician and explain the purpose of the visit.\n\n• Review the provided floor plans and identify the designated AP locations.\n\n• Confirm access to all relevant areas, including IDF/MDF rooms, ceilings, and other spaces required to evaluate cable pathways.\n\n• Confirm with the onsite contact whether AP equipment and installation materials have already been delivered or are available onsite.\n\n**2. EQUIPMENT &amp; EXISTING INFRASTRUCTURE VERIFICATION**\n\n• Verify whether the Wireless Access Points identified in the floor plans are physically available onsite.\n\n• If APs are onsite, document:\n\no Quantity and equipment models.\n\no Manufacturer, if identifiable.\n\no Equipment condition.\n\no Whether mounting hardware and accessories are available.\n\n• If APs are not onsite, document this and confirm whether the onsite contact has any information regarding equipment delivery or availability.\n\n• Identify any existing network racks, switches, patch panels, network cabling, and available infrastructure relevant to the proposed installation.\n\n• Verify the location and accessibility of the previously installed fiber-optic connection.\n\n• Document any additional infrastructure or equipment that may be required for the installation.\n\n**3. STRUCTURED CABLING SITE SURVEY**\n\n• Review the floor plans and identify the proposed cable termination points and AP locations.\n\n• Evaluate potential cable pathways from the network equipment room (MDF/IDF) to each designated AP location.\n\n• Inspect accessible ceiling spaces, conduits, cable trays, pathways, and other potential routing options.\n\n• Determine whether existing pathways can be reused or if new pathways will be required.\n\n• Identify potential obstacles or installation challenges, including:\n\no Inaccessible or restricted ceiling spaces.\n\no Walls or structures requiring penetration.\n\no Long cable routes or difficult access areas.\n\no Areas requiring lifts, ladders, or special equipment.\n\n• Estimate the approximate cable length required for each AP location.\n\n• Identify the proposed cable type and any additional materials required.\n\n• Document the proposed cable route and termination requirements for each location.\n\n**4. WIRELESS ACCESS POINT (AP) MOUNTING SURVEY**\n\n• Locate and verify each AP installation point identified on the provided floor plans.\n\n• Compare the proposed locations against actual site conditions.\n\n• Evaluate ceiling or wall mounting conditions and determine the appropriate mounting method.\n\n• Verify ceiling type, mounting surface, approximate installation height, and accessibility.\n\n• Identify whether existing mounting hardware can be reused or if new brackets or accessories will be required.\n\n• Determine whether any locations require special equipment, additional labor, or alternative mounting solutions.\n\n• Document any discrepancies between the floor plans and actual site conditions.\n\n• If a proposed AP location is not suitable, recommend an alternative location and explain the reason.\n\n**5. LABOR, MATERIALS &amp; INSTALLATION REQUIREMENTS**\n\nBased on the site inspection, provide an estimated breakdown of the work required for the future installation.\n\n• Estimate the number of new cable runs required.\n\n• Estimate the approximate cable length for each run.\n\n• Identify required materials, including:\n\no Ethernet cabling.\n\no Keystone jacks, patch panels, and termination hardware, if needed.\n\no Mounting brackets and AP accessories.\n\no Conduit, cable trays, or other pathway materials, if required.\n\no Cable management and labeling materials.\n\n• Identify any special tools or equipment required.\n\n• Estimate the labor hours required for the cabling and AP installation.\n\n• Identify any access restrictions, scheduling considerations, or dependencies that could affect the installation.\n\n**6. PHOTOS &amp; DOCUMENTATION**\n\nThe technician must provide a complete survey report for each location, including:\n\n• Photos of the MDF/IDF rooms, network racks, and relevant existing infrastructure.\n\n• Photos of the previously installed fiber-optic connection and its termination point.\n\n• Photos of each proposed AP mounting location.\n\n• Photos of potential cable pathways, ceiling spaces, and any obstacles.\n\n• Photos of AP equipment and accessories available onsite, if applicable.\n\n• Annotated floor plans identifying:\n\no Proposed AP locations.\n\no Proposed cable routes.\n\no Network equipment room locations.\n\no Any recommended changes to the proposed installation points.\n\n• Equipment inventory, including AP models and quantities, if available.\n\n• Estimated cable lengths and required materials.\n\n• Estimated labor hours and any special equipment requirements.\n\n• List of discrepancies, installation challenges, and outstanding items.\n\nAll findings must be clearly organized by location.\n\nIMPORTANT NOTES\n\n• This WO is strictly for site survey and information gathering.\n\n• Do not install, terminate, or modify any cabling.\n\n• Do not mount, connect, or configure any Wireless Access Points.\n\n• Do not modify the existing fiber-optic installation.\n\n• Any discrepancies between the provided floor plans and actual site conditions must be documented.\n\n• If AP equipment is unavailable onsite, report this clearly. Do not assume the equipment has been delivered.\n\n• Contact Splice before proceeding if any additional work is requested by the onsite contact.\n\nJOB DURATION: \\[4–6 hours estimated; confirm\\]\n\nEXPERIENCE\n\n• Experience performing structured cabling site surveys.\n\n• Familiarity with commercial network infrastructure, MDF/IDF rooms, and fiber-optic infrastructure.\n\n• Experience evaluating cable pathways and Ethernet cable installation requirements.\n\n• Familiarity with wireless access point installation, mounting methods, and common AP equipment.\n\n• Ability to interpret floor plans and prepare accurate installation estimates.\n\nREQUIRED TOOLS\n\n• Laptop.\n\n• Digital camera or smartphone for documentation.\n\n• Measuring tape or laser distance meter.\n\n• Flashlight.\n\n• Ladder, if required and permitted onsite.\n\n• Basic hand tools.\n\n• Any additional tools required to inspect accessible cable pathways.\n\nCONTACTS &amp; ESCALATION\n\nSee \"Confidential Information\" or the \"Contacts\" fields for onsite and Splice points of contact.\n\nSurveying technician is required to complete the cabling site survey form as an Excel file. Surveying technician is also required to supply a thorough work plan as to what the follow-up work will entail (scope of work, total hours, total materials).**Surveying technician is also required to provide this information within 72 hours of the site visit otherwise we will need to cancel the ticket, and find a new technician.**\n\n**PLEASE ONLY REQUEST THIS TICKET IF YOU WILL BE ABLE TO RETURN TO THE SITE FOR THE FOLLOW-UP WORK YOU ARE ESTIMATING FOR.**\n\n**REQUIREMENTS FOR A COMPLETED SURVEY:**\n\n- All tasks are completed as requested (please check with your service manager if you feel something does not apply or are unable to obtain).\n- You must complete the cabling survey document AS AN EXCEL FILE. If you take written notes that’s fine, but they must be transferred to the EXCEL FILE as it is required to officially complete that task.\n- We will need an itemized breakdown of the TIME AND MATERIALS required for any follow-up work as well as a DETAILED follow-up work plan. This would involve all man hours, itemized materials and costs, as well as tasks/steps to having the job completed properly.\n\n**JOB DURATION AND REQUIRED EXPERIENCE:**\n\nJob duration is expected to be 2-3 hours.\n\nTechnician will need to be proficient with low voltage cabling, cable tracing, toning, crimping, and testing. Will need to be well versed with cable management standards, materials (J-Hooks, wire clips, conduit), certifications, local codes, and other building requirements. Technician will need to understand network device connectivity between routers, firewalls, switches, servers and patch panels/punch downs, wall plates, keystones, and end user devices. Will need to have all tools and materials for testing or making new cables, installing face plates or keystones as required. Will need to be comfortable on ladders and lifts, entering and navigating attics and crawl spaces.\n\nTechnicians will need to be proficient with low voltage cabling as it pertains to fiber cabling, using detection, verification, certification testers. Will also need to have the tools and understand the usage of fault locators, light source, and power meters. Will also need to understand fiber optic assembly tools, splicers (splice or splicing tools), multimode and single mode connections, LC/SC ends and connections. Will need to be well versed with cable management standards, materials (J-Hooks, wire clips, conduit), certifications, local codes, and other building requirements. Technician will need to understand network device connectivity between routers, firewalls, switches, servers and patch panels/punch downs, wall plates, keystones, and end user devices. Will need to be comfortable on ladders and lifts, entering and navigating attics and crawl spaces.\n\n**REQUIRED TOOLS:**\n\n- Laptop with working power supply, ethernet port and Wi-Fi connectivity\n- Excel Software (to complete cabling spreadsheet)\n- Standard hand tools (screwdrivers, pliers, snips, etc.)\n- Label maker\n- Patch Cables\n- Cabling tools (crimpers, testers, toners, punch down tools)\n- Smartphone\n- Hot spot\n- Ladder (minimum 4-6 foot A-frame, will need to specify if different)\n- Measuring tape\n- Measuring Wheel\n- Fishing tape\n\n**As stated in the terms related to this work order, as well as the tasks outlined below, all deliverables and tasks must be uploaded and completed as requested. Please contact your Service Manager PRIOR to departing your location if any questions or issues with a particular deliverable/task. All must be uploaded and marked as WORK DONE no later than 24 hours after departing site. There could be financial penalties if this is not adhered to.**\n\n**MATERIALS:**\n\n- TBD\n\n**PARTS:**\n\n{You could put information or tracking numbers/pick-up locations for equipment that is being sent to a location or held for pick-up.}\n\n**IMPORTANT CONTACTS AND ESCALATION PATH:**\n\nCAN BE FOUND UNDER \"CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION\" OR IN THE \"CONTACTS\" FIELDS.\n\n**TASKS/DELIVERABLES**\n\nPLEASE FOLLOW THROUGH THE TASKS AND REQUESTED DELIVERABLES LISTED ON THE PORTAL. CONFIRM WITH YOUR SERVICE MANAGER IF A TASK MAY NOT BE APPLICABLE.\n\n**DRESS CODE:**\n\nBusiness casual attire (unless doing any low voltage work involving attic spaces/etc.). This would include a clean shirt with no logos, button down or Polo, khakis or jeans with no holes, dress shoes and/or sneakers with no flashy design.","actions":[]},"isRequestedWindow":false,"time":{"start":"2026-11-11T10:00","end":"2026-11-11T12:00"},"payRange":{"min":50,"max":100},"payType":"hourly","payStructure":null,"hourlyRate":50,"estLaborHours":2,"distance":58.700716303073676},"result":{"eligible":false,"counterOffer":null,"reason":"PAYMENT_BELOW_MINIMUM","rejectDetails":"Total pay $100 (countered: $120) is below minimum threshold $300"},"availableBlocks":[{"start":"2026-11-12T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T14:30:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-12T23:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-13T01:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-13T20:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-14T01:00:00.000Z"}],"busyBlocks":[{"start":"2026-11-12T14:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T23:30:00.000Z","summary":"Progitech"},{"start":"2026-11-12T22:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T23:00:00.000Z","summary":"Pay bills BoA + Amex"},{"start":"2026-11-11T05:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T05:00:00.000Z","summary":"Veterans Day"},{"start":"2026-11-10T21:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-11T00:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 ***Thermo Fisher | Network Cutover Assistance*** (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-17T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 The Preiss Company-05974911-Epik Cutover & Test (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-10T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-10T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 05636592 - 1013 Classic Rd | VTM CUT (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-16T21:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T00:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 Advance Auto Parts - Phone Swap - 4810 Raleigh  NC (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-17T17:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T20:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 Rollins/Orkin Pest Control - FortiGate Swap & edgebootPro installation - Raleigh, NC (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-16T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-16T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"[INC#: WO 2026 Nov 16 SITE#: RTP/DC3]   To Be Updated"},{"start":"2026-11-13T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-13T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"[INC#: 00740402 - ATT SITE#: RTP/DC3]   To Be Updated"},{"start":"2026-11-11T13:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-11T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"POS equipment install"},{"start":"2026-11-09T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-09T17:00:00.000Z","summary":"Dream Finders - Site Survey - Networking"}],"action":"SKIP","ts":1794266025839}
{"job":{"id":20336325,"platform":"FieldNation","company":"SpotOn","title":"POS Site Survey and Cabling Verification","description":{"html":"<strong>Overview</strong><ul><li>Seeking a technician with experience in POS installations, networking, and site surveys.</li><li>Technician is required to perform a pre-installation site inspection for a SpotOn POS deployment.</li><li>Estimated duration will be 1 hour</li></ul><strong>Dress Code</strong><ul><li>Business casual attire.</li><li>Professional appearance required when interacting with restaurant management and staff.</li></ul><strong>Tools Required</strong><ul><li>Network cable toner and tester</li><li>Laptop</li><li>Label maker or cable labels</li><li>Camera or smartphone for site photos</li><li>Internet speed testing capability</li><li>Basic hand tools as needed</li></ul><strong>Pre-Site Requirements</strong><ol><li>Review the Site Inspection form and all project documentation.</li><li>Contact the site Point of Contact (POC) to confirm the inspection date and time.</li><li>Review hardware counts and deployment requirements.</li><li>Familiarize yourself with SpotOn network and connectivity requirements.</li></ol><strong>On-Site Tasks</strong><ol><li>Check in through the work order and introduce yourself to the site manager.</li><li>Review equipment counts with the POC.</li><li>Walk the site and identify proposed locations for all POS equipment.</li><li>Document and record each device location.</li><li>Create a diagram showing placement of terminals, printers, payment devices, KDS screens, and other hardware.</li><li>Tone and test cabling for future printer and KDS locations.</li><li>Perform a hardwired internet speed test.</li><li>Verify all POS devices can reside on the same wired network.</li><li>Label POS cabling and network ports.</li><li>Document any site readiness issues, including missing cabling, power, or construction concerns.</li><li><strong>Complete the site inspection form here: </strong>cognitoforms.com/SPOTON3/SiteInspectionResponsesFieldNation</li></ol><strong>Required On-Site Deliverables</strong><ol><li><strong>Completed Site Inspection form.</strong></li><li>Photos showing Ethernet and power availability for all stations, printers, and KDS locations.</li><li>Network speed test results.</li><li>Site diagram showing all equipment locations.</li><li>Documentation of any equipment additions/removals or site readiness concerns.</li><li>Technician and client/POC signatures.</li><li>If additional cabling is required, please provide a quote for labor and materials for cabling on this work order</li></ol><strong>Escalation Process</strong><ul><li>If equipment counts do not match project documentation, contact the project coordinator.</li><li>Escalate any network, cabling, power, or construction concerns before leaving the site.</li><li>Escalate any questions that prevent completion of the inspection to the SpotOn implementation team.</li></ul><strong>Additional Hours &amp; Expenses Approval Policy</strong><ul><li>Additional labor, travel, or material expenses must be approved before being incurred.</li><li>Do not perform work outside the defined scope without approval.</li><li>Notify the project coordinator immediately if additional work is identified.</li></ul>","markdown":"**Overview**- Seeking a technician with experience in POS installations, networking, and site surveys.\n- Technician is required to perform a pre-installation site inspection for a SpotOn POS deployment.\n- Estimated duration will be 1 hour\n\n**Dress Code**- Business casual attire.\n- Professional appearance required when interacting with restaurant management and staff.\n\n**Tools Required**- Network cable toner and tester\n- Laptop\n- Label maker or cable labels\n- Camera or smartphone for site photos\n- Internet speed testing capability\n- Basic hand tools as needed\n\n**Pre-Site Requirements**1. Review the Site Inspection form and all project documentation.\n2. Contact the site Point of Contact (POC) to confirm the inspection date and time.\n3. Review hardware counts and deployment requirements.\n4. Familiarize yourself with SpotOn network and connectivity requirements.\n\n**On-Site Tasks**1. Check in through the work order and introduce yourself to the site manager.\n2. Review equipment counts with the POC.\n3. Walk the site and identify proposed locations for all POS equipment.\n4. Document and record each device location.\n5. Create a diagram showing placement of terminals, printers, payment devices, KDS screens, and other hardware.\n6. Tone and test cabling for future printer and KDS locations.\n7. Perform a hardwired internet speed test.\n8. Verify all POS devices can reside on the same wired network.\n9. Label POS cabling and network ports.\n10. Document any site readiness issues, including missing cabling, power, or construction concerns.\n11. **Complete the site inspection form here:** cognitoforms.com/SPOTON3/SiteInspectionResponsesFieldNation\n\n**Required On-Site Deliverables**1. **Completed Site Inspection form.**\n2. Photos showing Ethernet and power availability for all stations, printers, and KDS locations.\n3. Network speed test results.\n4. Site diagram showing all equipment locations.\n5. Documentation of any equipment additions/removals or site readiness concerns.\n6. Technician and client/POC signatures.\n7. If additional cabling is required, please provide a quote for labor and materials for cabling on this work order\n\n**Escalation Process**- If equipment counts do not match project documentation, contact the project coordinator.\n- Escalate any network, cabling, power, or construction concerns before leaving the site.\n- Escalate any questions that prevent completion of the inspection to the SpotOn implementation team.\n\n**Additional Hours &amp; Expenses Approval Policy**- Additional labor, travel, or material expenses must be approved before being incurred.\n- Do not perform work outside the defined scope without approval.\n- Notify the project coordinator immediately if additional work is identified.","actions":[]},"isRequestedWindow":false,"time":{"start":"2026-11-12T09:00","end":"2026-11-12T11:30"},"payRange":{"min":250,"max":250},"payType":"fixed","payStructure":null,"hourlyRate":0,"estLaborHours":2.5,"distance":61.01439598134681},"result":{"eligible":false,"counterOffer":null,"reason":"PAYMENT_BELOW_MINIMUM","rejectDetails":"Total pay $250 is below minimum threshold $300"},"availableBlocks":[{"start":"2026-11-12T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T14:30:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-12T23:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-13T01:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-13T20:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-14T01:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-16T20:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-16T21:30:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-17T00:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T01:00:00.000Z"}],"busyBlocks":[{"start":"2026-11-12T14:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T23:30:00.000Z","summary":"Progitech"},{"start":"2026-11-12T22:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T23:00:00.000Z","summary":"Pay bills BoA + Amex"},{"start":"2026-11-10T21:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-11T00:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 ***Thermo Fisher | Network Cutover Assistance*** (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-17T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 The Preiss Company-05974911-Epik Cutover & Test (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-10T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-10T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 05636592 - 1013 Classic Rd | VTM CUT (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-16T21:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T00:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 Advance Auto Parts - Phone Swap - 4810 Raleigh  NC (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-17T17:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T20:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 Rollins/Orkin Pest Control - FortiGate Swap & edgebootPro installation - Raleigh, NC (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-16T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-16T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"[INC#: WO 2026 Nov 16 SITE#: RTP/DC3]   To Be Updated"},{"start":"2026-11-13T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-13T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"[INC#: 00740402 - ATT SITE#: RTP/DC3]   To Be Updated"},{"start":"2026-11-11T13:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-11T20:00:00.000Z","summary":"POS equipment install"},{"start":"2026-11-09T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-09T17:00:00.000Z","summary":"Dream Finders - Site Survey - Networking"}],"action":"SKIP","ts":1794266097847}
{"job":{"id":"8404624827","platform":"WorkMarket","company":"Granite Telecommunications","title":"Dispatch for ticket #7127762 | NOC Repair  -  Offline Device","description":"Issue: Epik is offline.  Will need to complete a Console session to get device back online.  Make sure to have a USB → DB9 Female cable (PL2303 Chipset cannot be any kind of adapter)  Connect Epik to Laptop and have Anydesk ready.  Once up call into the NOC for Console session.  Verify all 4 Elevators are working before leaving site  <br><br>NOC ticket: 7127762 <br>SN: 000069920004<br>Scope Of Work:<br>*** IMPORTANT: Tech must call into Work Market to check in and out upon arrival and before departing the site. ***<br>***Tech will call into Granite NOC at 844.492.2557 opt. 4 , then opt 1 to start the scope of work.***<br><br>PLEASE NOTE: There is no voicemail for the IW call queue. If you cannot get through to the NOC, pressing option 1 will prompt a callback from the next available NOC tech.<br><br>IMPORTANT: Tech must bring and have ready a USB → DB9 Female cable and a USB → Mini-USB serial cable for console access.<br><br>Device Offline Template: <br><br>1.Locate EPIK Edge device.<br>a.Take clear pictures of all light statuses and confirm that the Green power LED is lit. <br>2.Confirm LTE paddles are positioned away from each other at a 45 degree angle. <br>a.Reboot device if paddle position was changed and confirm with NOC if issue has been resolved.<br>3.Confirm if there is an ethernet connection connected to eth0/1. <br>a.If so, remove ethernet connection and reboot device. Confirm with NOC if issue has been resolved.<br>b.If device comes online over LTE with ethernet connection removed, share “Granite EPIK Network Configuration” document with customer’s IT team to confirm network device is configured to handoff an IP via DHCP and all ports and Ips are properly whitelisted. <br>4.If device remains offline, perform the following actions and have NOC escalate to EPIK Engineering to setup a loal console session for further investigation: <br>a.Connect Serial cable to Lap top and Epik,  DB9 for Black box,  Mini serial for Silve <br>b.Must be correct cables with PL2303 Chipset cannot be any kind of adapter<br>c.Open Anydesk on Laptop and Call into NOC for console Session <br>5.EPIK Engineering team will further advise on further steps.","isRequestedWindow":false,"time":{"start":"2026-11-12T08:00","end":"2026-11-12T11:00"},"payRange":{"min":65,"max":195},"payType":"hourly","payStructure":null,"hourlyRate":65,"estLaborHours":3,"distance":4.3},"result":{"eligible":false,"counterOffer":null,"reason":"SLOT_UNAVAILABLE","rejectDetails":"📅 No in-hours counter slot available\nRequested: Thu, Nov 12, 8:00 AM\nChecked: Thu, Nov 12, Fri, Nov 13, Mon, Nov 16 · 9:00 AM–8:00 PM\nNo block long enough was found."},"availableBlocks":[{"start":"2026-11-12T14:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T14:30:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-12T23:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-13T01:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-13T20:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-14T01:00:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-16T20:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-16T21:30:00.000Z"},{"start":"2026-11-17T00:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T01:00:00.000Z"}],"busyBlocks":[{"start":"2026-11-12T14:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T23:30:00.000Z","summary":"Progitech"},{"start":"2026-11-12T22:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-12T23:00:00.000Z","summary":"Pay bills BoA + Amex"},{"start":"2026-11-10T21:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-11T00:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 ***Thermo Fisher | Network Cutover Assistance*** (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-17T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 The Preiss Company-05974911-Epik Cutover & Test (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-10T15:00:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-10T18:00:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 05636592 - 1013 Classic Rd | VTM CUT (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-16T21:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T00:30:00.000Z","summary":"🔧 Advance Auto Parts - Phone Swap - 4810 Raleigh  NC (WM)"},{"start":"2026-11-17T17:30:00.000Z","end":"2026-11-17T20:3